WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:18.420
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies and&nbsp;
germs. This is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.560
episode of The Tim Ferriss Show, where it is&nbsp;
my job to deconstruct world class performers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.420
from all different disciplines. Could be chess,&nbsp;
could be military. In this case it is games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:34.680
all things gaming. My guest today is Elan Lee.&nbsp;
You can find them on Twitter at ElanLee. Elan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.700
is the co-creator and chief executive officer&nbsp;
of Exploding Kittens. Maybe you've heard of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.760
a leading gaming and entertainment&nbsp;
company. Under his leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.320
Exploding Kittens has expanded its portfolio&nbsp;
to nearly 30 different games with more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.240
20 million games sold in more than 50&nbsp;
countries since its founding in 2015.&nbsp;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.220
Before founding Exploding Kittens, Lee was the&nbsp;
chief design officer at Xbox Entertainment Studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.360
where he led the interactive entertainment&nbsp;
portfolio. Prior to that, he was the founder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.460
and chief creative officer of Fourth Wall Studios&nbsp;
and co-founder of 42 Entertainment. He began his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.800
career at Microsoft Games Studios as a lead&nbsp;
designer on the original Xbox. Lee has won a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.360
Primetime Emmy for the series Dirty Work, game&nbsp;
innovator of the year for Exploding Kittens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.740
a Peabody Award for the world's first&nbsp;
alternate reality game The Beast, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.140
an IndieCade Trailblazer Award for a distinguished&nbsp;
career in interactive entertainment, among others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.980
Elan, welcome to the show. Nice to see you.
Elan Lee: Thanks. It's really good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.760
to see you too. Happy to be here.
Tim Ferriss: So to paint a picture for folks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:40.740
downstairs I have what should function as a&nbsp;
dining table, completely covered in games of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:48.360
every imaginable type, and the floor is further&nbsp;
littered. There are dice everywhere. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.660
blank dice everywhere. Cards, there are blank&nbsp;
cards. So I'm in the midst of immersing myself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:02:01.680
all things, specifically tabletop gaming. I want&nbsp;
to thank you because of all the games tested so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:11.640
far, one game has stood out as a friend favorite,&nbsp;
and that is Poetry for Neanderthals. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.950
people out there who will catch me on Neandertals,&nbsp;
and fine, I'll say it both ways for you.&nbsp;

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.227
Elan Lee: Yeah, we asked for that&nbsp;
one naming that. Fair enough.&nbsp;

00:02:18.227 --> 00:02:22.860
Tim Ferriss: Where I'm hoping we could start&nbsp;
because I've listened to quite a few interviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:31.020
with you and there are other places where some&nbsp;
of your bio has been covered in I think a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.460
very good fashion. I recommend people listen&nbsp;
to Think Like A Game Designer in the episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:41.520
that Justin Gary did with you as well. But why&nbsp;
don't we start, because I found a video online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:48.420
which was you interviewing two game designers who&nbsp;
worked on Hand-To-Hand Wombat. I found that short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:55.320
conversation so incredibly helpful with the nitty&nbsp;
gritty of seeing how the sausage is made. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.780
hoping that perhaps we could start with Poetry&nbsp;
For Neanderthals as a case study from the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:07.200
inception, and you could just walk us through it.
Elan Lee: Yeah, absolutely. So Poetry For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.760
Neanderthals, it didn't even have a name. Of&nbsp;
course, no game has a name when you start, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:19.740
that one came to me from some friends. I met Jacob&nbsp;
and Francesca at this conference, and they came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.960
me and they're like, "Okay, you're the Exploding&nbsp;
Kittens guy. We've got this really cool idea. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:31.740
if there was a game where you had to learn a new&nbsp;
language and you're really bad at that language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.640
You're in a foreign land, everyone speaks the&nbsp;
language except you and you've got to try to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:43.200
by?" They said, "The way that we can simulate you&nbsp;
not speaking the correct language is you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.780
restriction [that] every sentence you speak, every&nbsp;
word in that sentence, must be a single syllable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:54.720
word." They give me some examples. They're like,&nbsp;
"Okay, so now listen, you Elan, you have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:00.420
I'm really hungry. I want to go to the store,&nbsp;
but there's a dragon at the store that's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:07.020
scary and I don't know what to do. Go."
I'm like, "Okay, okay, sure. Me want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:13.440
go get food." Immediately I realized, oh, I sound&nbsp;
like a caveman. That's kind of fun. You're right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:19.740
you have simulated this environment where I don't&nbsp;
speak the right language. We started working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:25.080
together, and it took about a year of playing&nbsp;
around. We got another designer involved, a guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:32.220
named Brian Spence, and realized there's something&nbsp;
super fun here. There is a core gameplay loop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.840
There's something that every time people touch&nbsp;
it, every time they play around with it, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.700
start laughing. They start wanting to tell their&nbsp;
friends about it. But holy crap, those sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:48.420
they are a mouthful. We took the sentences down&nbsp;
to five or six words, still too much. We took him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.680
down to three words, still too much. Took him down&nbsp;
to two words, still too much. We finally realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:58.380
why are we complicating this? What if the entire&nbsp;
game was, get your friend to say this word using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.480
only single syllable words? And that was it.
Once we realized that we were bringing baggage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:10.380
through for no reason, the game got really simple,&nbsp;
really fun. My co-founder and business partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:14.580
Matt Inman, who's the creator of The Oatmeal,&nbsp;
he took one look at it and said, "We're calling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:21.240
this thing Poetry For Neanderthals." And suddenly&nbsp;
we had a game. It was really those steps in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.040
order that let us push that thing out the door.
Tim Ferriss: And could I just maybe give a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.380
layman's take so people can envision what this&nbsp;
game looks like. So I played it with two other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.360
friends. It was the three of us, and actually we&nbsp;
did a lot of trial and error. So I think we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:43.020
did some experimental work just in terms of how&nbsp;
to optimize for three people. But imagine that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:49.980
have one person who is the caveman or cavewoman,&nbsp;
they have these cards and you flip over the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:55.380
and at the top you have "Ghost," let's just say,&nbsp;
as an example. And that will get you one point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:01.080
if you can give cues in these monosyllabic words.
Elan Lee: Only you can see that word, nobody else.&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.580
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's right. Only you&nbsp;
can see the word. And so you might say "Dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:13.440
man," and you start — it starts to get hard&nbsp;
very quickly. And then if you get that right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.360
now you can say the word "Ghost." You're allowed&nbsp;
to say that. And so the three point, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:24.300
the three point option below that might be "Ghost&nbsp;
town," let's just say. And there is an hourglass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.280
that is turned over, which gives you I think it's&nbsp;
maybe 60 seconds to try, something like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:35.040
to rack up as many points as possible. Usually&nbsp;
you do this in teams, if you break the rules and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:44.340
you say something like "Casper," there's somebody&nbsp;
with an inflatable club, which does not hurt, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:50.700
whacks you on the head with this thing or I guess&nbsp;
on some other body part. And so with three guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:56.580
adolescent at heart, even though this is designed&nbsp;
I think for ages seven and up, we just got hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:01.500
and hours of endless fun out of this thing.
It was so much fun because you also have these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:06.240
ridiculous things that come up. My brother's&nbsp;
wife, so my sister-in-law was trying — she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.400
had teeth and she said — no, no, she had "Mouth."&nbsp;
"Mouth" was the one-point word. And she said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:14.400
"Teeth house." And he was like, "Teeth house?"&nbsp;
The stuff that comes out can be really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.980
really funny. And it's very easy to get&nbsp;
up and running quickly, which is what made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:25.500
this I think more attractive than some of the&nbsp;
other games I bought at game shops, which were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.980
a much heavier lift on the way in.
And I want to just as a way of setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.680
the table, tell people part of the&nbsp;
reason I was excited to have you on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:41.220
and we chatted a bit before recording, and you&nbsp;
and I are aligned on I'm sure quite a few things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:46.620
but one is helping people, and I'll include&nbsp;
myself in that group to spend less time in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:54.000
front of screens. And games are a very easy way to&nbsp;
do that if you find the right games and you choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.360
the right tool to suit your personality.&nbsp;
And those are the friends that you have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.884
So I jumped in and gave a TED Talk there for&nbsp;
a second. So thanks for putting up with that.&nbsp;

00:08:03.884 --> 00:08:07.260
Elan Lee: Well, but hold on, let me just&nbsp;
interject. What you just described. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:13.080
you're hired, that was the perfect commercial.&nbsp;
What you just described is a game that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:18.300
necessarily entertaining, but a game that&nbsp;
allowed those three people — you included — to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.980
entertain each other. And that's music to my&nbsp;
ears. That means, okay, success. We built a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:27.420
cool game there. That was the goal. We hit it. I&nbsp;
didn't even have to be in the room to facilitate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.800
that. You just had exactly the experience I&nbsp;
wanted you to have. I'm thrilled. Absolutely.&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:40.800
Tim Ferriss: So let me dive into a few of the&nbsp;
stages. You met the game designers, who you named,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:49.140
and what led you to want to work with them?&nbsp;
Because you must personally and as a company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:54.540
I would imagine get pitched a lot of new games.
Elan Lee: A lot. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:08:54.540 --> 00:09:00.840
Tim Ferriss: What was it besides the concept,&nbsp;
because ideas are worth something, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:06.900
not worth as much as people like to think. So what&nbsp;
was it that led you to want to commit to working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:11.700
with them and in what capacity was that? And&nbsp;
what happened over that year? Was it simply that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.280
everyone had a lot of projects they were juggling&nbsp;
and that had to fit in the gaps or something else?&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.660
Elan Lee: So we got to take a little&nbsp;
bit of a look at the anatomy of a game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:29.220
What that means is I believe the most important&nbsp;
part of a game is I don't have a great term for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:33.480
it. Actually I have no training in game&nbsp;
design, so I make all this stuff up along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.960
as I go. So bear with me. But at a game school —
Tim Ferriss: I think you're doing fine in life.&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.100
Elan Lee: Thanks.
Tim Ferriss: Making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.480
it up as you go. So keep doing what you're doing.
Elan Lee: Yeah, yeah. Will do. All right. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.640
at the essence of any game is what I at least&nbsp;
call the core gameplay loop. And that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.440
thing that you're going to do over and over and&nbsp;
over again. So in the case of Exploding Kittens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:58.020
the core gameplay loop is Russian roulette with a&nbsp;
deck of cards. There's a few bad cards in there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:02.820
Draw a card, hope it's not the bad one. You&nbsp;
survive to see another day. Core gameplay loop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:09.480
UNO, right? Classic game. Everybody knows UNO.&nbsp;
UNO, if I had to describe the core gameplay loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:13.800
I would say it's a lock and key mechanism. And&nbsp;
all that means is you have a hand of cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:19.800
everyone's a key. As soon as you play a key on top&nbsp;
of a lock, which is in the center of the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.540
it becomes a new lock for the next player.
That's the core gameplay loop. In that case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:28.620
it's numbers and colors, but still it's just&nbsp;
locks and keys and playing one converts one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:37.860
So that concept of finding a core gameplay loop&nbsp;
that's compelling and interesting is really hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:45.840
Really, really hard. And the ones that I'm most&nbsp;
attracted to when I hear them is core gameplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.880
loops that are not actually entertaining. Core&nbsp;
gameplay loops that make the people you're playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.440
with entertaining. Because I really think that's&nbsp;
at the heart of games. When I think back to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.300
childhood and playing games with my brothers and&nbsp;
sisters growing up, I don't remember any of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.960
games because I don't think I should remember any&nbsp;
of the games. The games were not important. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:09.180
the tool set. Those games are tools for me to&nbsp;
have fun with my siblings. And I remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:15.060
fun. I remember the laughter. I remember the&nbsp;
secret alliances. I remember all that stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.940
because the games had really strong core gameplay&nbsp;
loops that allowed me to have those interactions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.820
All right, sorry, big rabbit hole there, but —
Tim Ferriss: Love rabbit holes.&nbsp;

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.520
Elan Lee: We'll try to keep it short. Right. Okay.
Tim Ferriss: So that's what long-form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:27.600
podcasts are for.
Elan Lee: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.820
fair enough. All right. So the next thing. So for&nbsp;
Poetry, when Jacob and Francesca came to me with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.900
this concept, I didn't think it was a good game.
Tim Ferriss: And just for clarity, were they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:41.750
friends, were they out of the blue? Were they&nbsp;
working for the company in part-time or full-time?&nbsp;

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:46.320
Elan Lee: So this was at a conference called ORD&nbsp;
Camp in Chicago. It's an invite-only conference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.780
So very few people there. I show up, crazy&nbsp;
introvert, I'm scared out of my mind to talk to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:57.060
people, but there's only a hundred people there.&nbsp;
So anybody who comes up to me and says hello,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.500
I'm like, okay, there's something cool about&nbsp;
this person. Otherwise, they wouldn't be here.&nbsp;

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.924
Tim Ferriss: And there's nowhere&nbsp;
to hide. There's nowhere to hide.&nbsp;

00:12:04.924 --> 00:12:08.460
Elan Lee: Yeah, there's really nowhere to&nbsp;
hide. So when they approached me and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.320
"Hey, can we talk to you?" I just thought, I need&nbsp;
to give everybody here the benefit of the doubt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:18.540
I need to get over my shyness, I need to talk to&nbsp;
them. And it turns out it was a great idea because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:25.920
what they showed me, like I said, was not a fun&nbsp;
game, but a really interesting core gameplay loop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:32.040
This idea that communication is easy, if&nbsp;
you reduce it down to monosyllabic words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:38.820
communication becomes really challenging and&nbsp;
you need your friend's help to get you from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:44.580
that caveman talk back to regular English.
Tim Ferriss: So over that, then, year period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:51.540
of time, what is happening, just to come back&nbsp;
to the anatomy of the development of the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.440
How do you refine? Do you play test? What&nbsp;
is play testing, all that kind of stuff?&nbsp;

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:00.480
Elan Lee: One of the biggest assets that we&nbsp;
have at our company, and the thing that took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:06.600
me seven years to build is this program called&nbsp;
the Kitty Test Pilots. And this is a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.680
families that have reached out to us saying,&nbsp;
we love your games, tell us when the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:16.260
one comes out. And we respond, great, want to&nbsp;
test some prototypes for us? And we regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:22.500
send them out very, very half baked ideas.
And we asked them just to send us feedback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:27.240
So what happened over the next year? So we took&nbsp;
that idea, I showed it to some friends, I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:31.920
okay, what if you had to translate these sentences&nbsp;
using only single syllable words? And what came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.860
back was, holy crap, this is cumbersome. This&nbsp;
is so hard and there's not enough time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:42.540
my turn took eight minutes, and what the hell&nbsp;
is that? And we all this really bad feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:47.760
but I knew that the core gameplay loop was&nbsp;
really solid. So that's when we went down to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:51.300
okay, what if the sentences were only five&nbsp;
words long? What if we reduced the timer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.900
two minutes, then one minute? I think&nbsp;
the final is actually only 45 seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.160
Don't hold me to that. Something in that range.
Tim Ferriss: Something. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:05.160
Elan Lee: But we started to realize I'm getting&nbsp;
better and better results. The fewer words they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:10.800
have to do at once, but they want more time to do&nbsp;
a bunch more words. So eventually we got down to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:16.320
all right, let's try out two things. One, you only&nbsp;
have to do one word at a time. Let's see if that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:22.380
interesting. And two, what does punishment&nbsp;
mean? Because everybody laughs when you use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:26.760
a word with more than one syllable. But then&nbsp;
it kind of ended. It was setting up for a joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:31.260
but the punchline never came. And so we thought,&nbsp;
all right, we'll put a buzzer in there. Or there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:38.460
a word you shout out, and I'm pretty sure it was&nbsp;
Matt with his just perfect pitch sense of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:43.560
said, well, it's a caveman game, so give him a&nbsp;
club and let them bonk each other over the head.&nbsp;

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:49.440
Tim Ferriss: I'm sure general counsel loved that.
Elan Lee: Oh man, you would not believe how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.900
lawyers hate us. Holy crap. Endless,&nbsp;
endless conversations. But they made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.900 --> 00:15:00.960
us in the instructions, they made us say, "Hit the&nbsp;
other person over the head softly." They insisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:07.320
on that. And so we changed it without asking. We&nbsp;
put it to "soft-ish" over the head, and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:12.120
our version of a compromise. Anyway, when we talk&nbsp;
about the anatomy of a game though, we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:17.340
about that core game play loop. Okay, we've really&nbsp;
got it now. One word, get through as many one word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:23.760
cards as you can, as fast as you can. You've got&nbsp;
a few seconds to do so. And now we start to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:31.020
the surroundings around the game. We need&nbsp;
to pick really funny words that we know are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:36.600
going to elicit funny responses. "Teeth" is&nbsp;
really great. "Teeth" was calculated. "Teeth"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:40.620
is not just a word that shows up in that game.
We had to think, all right, what are words where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:45.600
when you try to describe them, you're going&nbsp;
to start to stumble and the places you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:52.440
to go yield really funny results. And things&nbsp;
like "teeth" start to show up in the game. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.520
then we started thinking about punishment and we&nbsp;
started thinking about the “no stick” it's called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:01.860
because everything in the game is just single&nbsp;
syllables and on and on it went until we had our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:08.760
core game loop surrounded by this really beautiful&nbsp;
kind of menagerie of things that enhanced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:14.520
experience and made it so like, okay, you can&nbsp;
start playing this game. Every single element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:19.800
is going to push you towards that core gameplay&nbsp;
loop. And the core gameplay loop is going to rely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:25.320
it's the fuel running that engine, that loop&nbsp;
running over and over again is the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:32.100
players. And once we hit that and this beautiful,&nbsp;
elegant, fast loop, that's when we start throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:36.660
that thing in a box and pushing it out the door.
Tim Ferriss: Chipping it out. So could you give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:41.880
another example or two of core gameplay&nbsp;
loop? Because on one hand it sounds like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:47.880
there could be a core gameplay loop that is&nbsp;
almost like a theme much like you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:54.360
the Hollywood pitch of say Alien, which is Jaws&nbsp;
in space, right? So there's something very simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.360 --> 00:17:01.980
which encapsulates the melding of two ideas that&nbsp;
make something interesting. But it could also be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:07.740
game mechanic, which I know much less about. Could&nbsp;
you give another example of a core gameplay loop?&nbsp;

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:13.200
Elan Lee: I love dissecting games to find these.&nbsp;
So I'll start with one of my own. So let's look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:17.940
at Exploding Kittens. I described it really&nbsp;
briefly before as just Russian roulette with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:22.560
a deck of cards. That's not accurate. If we want&nbsp;
to talk about the actual core gameplay loop, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:28.800
that game is Russian roulette with a deck of cards&nbsp;
where you get to decide where to place the bullet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:33.960
And that is super important that actually&nbsp;
the very first time we tested the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:37.980
it was just, here's a deck of cards, there's&nbsp;
a few bullets, and we didn't even know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:41.700
to call it at the time. There's a few bullets&nbsp;
in this deck. We're just going to take turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:46.140
drawing. Someone draws a bullet, you're dead.&nbsp;
Everybody continues on without you. That game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:49.560
was no fun at all. And the reason it was no fun&nbsp;
is because it's us playing against the game.&nbsp;

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:56.340
The game has all the burden of entertaining us,&nbsp;
absolutely uninteresting. We introduced these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.340 --> 00:18:01.140
other cards into the game called defuse cards, and&nbsp;
all the defuse card did was it said, "Look, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:06.720
draw that bullet instead of dying, you can put&nbsp;
it back in the deck anywhere you want in secret."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:13.020
Now we've got a game because now it's not —&nbsp;
it's not actually Russian roulette. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:19.080
a random game. You have to survive me, you have&nbsp;
to outthink me. I just put this thing somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:22.980
in the deck and now it's your turn to go. Did&nbsp;
I put it on top? Maybe you don't want to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:26.340
that card. Did I put it on the bottom? Maybe&nbsp;
you don't want to draw that card. Did I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:30.240
that you knew that I knew that you knew that I&nbsp;
didn't put it on the top and so you skipped your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:35.640
turn and drew the second one down? Well, maybe&nbsp;
I knew that. Right? Suddenly, the game fades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:40.740
away and I'm playing against you. And that's&nbsp;
the core gameplay loop of Exploding Kittens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:45.900
and I believe why it's been so successful.
Tim Ferriss: So we're going to bounce all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:51.360
the place, which is I guess I'm going to make it a&nbsp;
feature and not a bug of my style of conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:57.900
but let's chat if you're open to it about&nbsp;
Kickstarter for a moment. Because I know a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:03.960
lot of people outside of games specifically will&nbsp;
still be interested in Kickstarter or crowdfunding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:11.220
in general. So you, I believe at one point,&nbsp;
had a fraud alert email printed out and framed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:16.800
and I think somewhere the limit was set like&nbsp;
10,000 and maybe it got bumped like 50,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:20.160
for this new bank account for things.&nbsp;
What was your initial target for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:24.240
Kickstarter campaign and where did you end up?
Elan Lee: The initial target was we were trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:30.960
to raise $10,000 in 30 days, and we set that&nbsp;
alert. The bank would let us deposit a check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:36.780
up to $50,000. They were being very generous,&nbsp;
and the check that we tried to deposit at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:43.020
end of those 30 days was almost $9 million.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Yeah. So I have specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:51.660
questions around how you did things differently&nbsp;
and you had some very strong advantages going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:58.380
in. You had your background. You had Matt and The&nbsp;
Oatmeal and all the followers of The Oatmeal. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:04.260
you had a deck that was loaded in&nbsp;
a sense, but still ended up being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:08.460
maybe still is the most backed in terms&nbsp;
of number of backers, Kickstarter project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:12.060
or at least at the time that was the case.
Elan Lee: Still the case by a lot, actually. So in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:17.040
order to talk about Kickstarter, we have to talk&nbsp;
about two things. One is alternate reality games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:26.040
and the other is The Oatmeal. So alternate reality&nbsp;
games, my background being sort of trained at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:32.340
Microsoft and at the Xbox, and learning about&nbsp;
communities and the appeal of games and why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:38.520
play games together, I then went off and started&nbsp;
with some friends, this company to build alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:41.760
reality games. And all you really need to know&nbsp;
about alternate reality games, although I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:50.820
talk about them for a long time, is the central&nbsp;
premise is together you are stronger. Together as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:57.000
a community, you can do stuff, you can become&nbsp;
extraordinary versions of yourselves. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:03.300
games are set up to deliver a story out in the&nbsp;
real world that is convoluted and you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:09.060
piece it back together. It's like my mentor Jordan&nbsp;
Weisman likes to call it, internet archeology.&nbsp;

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:17.100
So archeology is you find all the little bits&nbsp;
and pieces of the vase. You put it together in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:21.660
order to figure out what it is about that society,&nbsp;
how that society lived, how those people lived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:28.380
Alternate reality games are the same thing. We&nbsp;
write a beautiful story, start to finish with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:33.180
very compelling narrative, and then we break it&nbsp;
up into little pieces and we hide those pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:38.880
everywhere in the real world, on the internet, on&nbsp;
phone lines, fax lines, live actors, everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:45.180
And you the audience working together because&nbsp;
you're stronger together go and perform that feat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:49.800
of archeology, find all the pieces, put them back&nbsp;
together, look at your beautiful digital vase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:58.560
and then learn the story based on what you found.
So I built a lot of those and I was really trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:05.280
over and over again, really beaten into my psyche&nbsp;
community first, community first. They're so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:09.300
smarter than you are. Learn to entertain&nbsp;
a crowd. That's what this is all about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:14.580
All right. We decided to launch this&nbsp;
Kickstarter campaign. We had this really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:20.760
compelling hook. We knew the game was really good.&nbsp;
We put up the page and the very first thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:27.540
happens is The Oatmeal goes into full effect. Matt&nbsp;
has spent at that point about a decade building an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:33.647
audience, earning their trust, convincing them&nbsp;
that he does just really high-quality work.&nbsp;

00:22:33.647 --> 00:22:37.440
Tim Ferriss: And for those who don't know,&nbsp;
could you just explain what The Oatmeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:41.820
so at that point, what it looked like?
Elan Lee: Yeah. So at that point, The Oatmeal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:51.360
was and still is a webpage, theoatmeal.com. And&nbsp;
Matt has spent 10 years writing short form comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:54.824
one panel, sometimes up to four panel&nbsp;
comics, sometimes very few long form comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.824 --> 00:23:00.000
or I should say long form pieces where he tells&nbsp;
stories about his childhood or he — it's a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.060
social commentary and they're hilarious. They're&nbsp;
beautiful. Some of them have won Eisner awards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:13.560
They will make you cry. Matt is truly one of the&nbsp;
most talented artists and comedians I've ever met.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:20.160
And yeah, he's a guy who's just so much smarter&nbsp;
than I am. It's an incredible collaboration. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:24.900
got to meet him through a mutual friend. I pitched&nbsp;
this game to him. We talked about it. He said he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:30.300
really like to help. And off we went. And when&nbsp;
we launched the Kickstarter campaign, honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:33.890
it was only three weeks after I met him.
Tim Ferriss: Wow. That's fast.&nbsp;

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:37.670
Elan Lee: Yeah, really fast. We built this —
Tim Ferriss: Didn't realize it was that fast.&nbsp;

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:41.760
Elan Lee: We built it fast because we had nothing&nbsp;
to lose. We didn't think it would be a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:45.702
success. We just thought, this feels really good.&nbsp;
We don't have to overthink this. Let's just go.&nbsp;

00:23:45.702 --> 00:23:51.120
Tim Ferriss: Now, is it fair to say that also at&nbsp;
that point you had the game mechanics refined and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.120 --> 00:24:00.960
Matt was bringing in a lot of the sort of artistic&nbsp;
comedic flare in terms of the back hair cards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:08.160
the artwork. So you're effectively adding art to&nbsp;
game mechanics that were ready to go, but kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:12.900
lacked an artistic vehicle. Is that fair to say?
Elan Lee: That's totally fair. There are 56 cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:17.760
in the game. And so Matt's task was not to&nbsp;
design the game, although we both worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:26.040
hard to refine it. Matt's task was write 56&nbsp;
one-panel jokes. And he did. You flip through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:30.767
that deck and the first thing you're going to do&nbsp;
is just laugh. I mean, it's such beautiful art.&nbsp;

00:24:30.767 --> 00:24:32.580
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Elan Lee: Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:37.260
so back to our story. We launched the Kickstarter&nbsp;
campaign and Matt posts about it. And he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:42.240
"For the first time ever, I've done a game. I hope&nbsp;
you like it." And millions of people showed up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:48.840
literally millions of views to that Kickstarter&nbsp;
page. And we got funded, we were trying to raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:55.320
$10,000. We got funded in seven minutes. I mean,&nbsp;
it was out of control. And our first day we made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:59.340
a million dollars and our second day we had $2&nbsp;
million, and our third day we had $3 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:06.360
And it was just like, this is a runaway train. We&nbsp;
have certainly caught lightning in a bottle. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:11.340
have unleashed the full potential of The Oatmeal.&nbsp;
Here's finally a way to productize that incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:21.120
brand. Welcome Exploding Kittens to the world. But&nbsp;
then after the first week, it fell off a cliff.&nbsp;

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:27.360
And the reason is just because everyone who Matt&nbsp;
could reach, who was interested in this thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:33.000
had taken a look, either made a purchase decision&nbsp;
or not, and that was it. The tank was empty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:40.500
So I sat down with him and we're like, well, we've&nbsp;
got two choices. We can either just sort of ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:46.260
off into the sunset and say, we made $4 million&nbsp;
bucks. That's incredible. We were trying to raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:52.740
$10,000. Let's just take a bow and we're done. Or&nbsp;
we should try to push this thing a little bit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.740 --> 00:26:07.140
just see what else is possible on Kickstarter.
At the time, the way that Kickstarter worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:11.820
the kind of only lever you had were these things&nbsp;
called stretch goals. And the way a stretch goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:17.820
works is you say, "Look, I've got this product.&nbsp;
I'm going to charge you 20 bucks for it, and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:24.000
going to try to raise $10,000. But if we raise&nbsp;
$20,000 for free, everybody gets three more bonus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:31.020
cards. And if we raise $50,000, you get a carrying&nbsp;
case. And if we raise $100,000, gold-plated cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:35.520
whatever it is." That was the tool you had.&nbsp;
"Give us more money, we'll give you more shit."&nbsp;

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:53.820
We kind of thought, there's got to be&nbsp;
something else we can do. There's got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:57.900
be something else. And I suddenly like my&nbsp;
eyes went wide and I realized, holy crap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:03.900
I've been training for this moment my whole damn&nbsp;
life. We need to activate the community. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:09.600
of thinking of this as crowdfunding, let's&nbsp;
think of this as CROWDfunding, right? And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:15.180
we did stretch goals just like everybody else,&nbsp;
but instead of tying it to money, we tied it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:20.820
just insane shit that we could ask the&nbsp;
backers to do. We basically said, "Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:25.140
we're going to throw a party and everybody's&nbsp;
invited. Instead of giving us more money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:30.480
we're done with money. Instead, we've got this&nbsp;
character in our game called Tacocat, half cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:37.620
half taco. Show us a picture of a real tacocat.&nbsp;
And if you do, if 10 people do that, we'll throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.520
in 10 extra cards." And that's a stretch goal.
"And you know what would be funny? What about give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:45.840
us a picture of 10 Batmans in a hot tub, whatever&nbsp;
the hell that means, we want to see it. Somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:51.300
does that, we'll give you a fancy carrying&nbsp;
case." And we just went nuts. We wrote these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:59.520
challenges that were insane and funny, and&nbsp;
basically said, "We just want to have fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:03.840
please have fun with us. This is going to&nbsp;
be — we're only here for another 20 days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:09.900
so why don't we just celebrate the whole way&nbsp;
through?" And the audience jumped at it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:15.360
they did everything we asked. They took those&nbsp;
pictures and they wrote poems, they filmed videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:19.860
they went out on the streets, they met each&nbsp;
other, they had parties together, they ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:25.860
pizza. All the fun stuff because we basically&nbsp;
said, "Money doesn't matter anymore, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:30.360
just have fun." And it was a great invitation.
Tim Ferriss: All right. So many places I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:37.920
go with this and of course, all of this alternate&nbsp;
reality training for this sudden IRL Olympics that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:39.480
is brought to bear in Kickstarter.
Elan Lee: That's right.&nbsp;

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:45.840
Tim Ferriss: The first is just to put in&nbsp;
context the "money doesn't matter" comment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:50.820
because it seems like at the time, you were around&nbsp;
four and you made additional millions of dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:58.020
Is that because there was a pre-order button&nbsp;
on the page, and as buzz built, even though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:04.740
tiers weren't predicated on people spending more&nbsp;
money, you had more new orders coming in as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:09.420
buzz around these challenges and so on traveled?
Elan Lee: That's exactly right. We didn't ask for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:15.180
more money, we just said, "This is the&nbsp;
page where we're going to use this page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:18.900
as mission control. This is where we're&nbsp;
going to put out all the new challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:23.340
this is where we're going to tell you what you've&nbsp;
earned when those challenges are complete." None&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:27.840
of those challenges, again, have anything to do&nbsp;
with money. But because it's a Kickstarter page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:31.740
there's also a back this project button right&nbsp;
there. And so the dollars kept rolling in.&nbsp;

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:38.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I love this because you're&nbsp;
doing what I think is so beautiful when it works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

