don't need to keep track of all of it because&nbsp;
really you're saying, "If we get 10 Batmans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:37.860
in a tub and we get 10 of those, unlock."
Elan Lee: Yeah, exactly. We tracked it very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:43.740
very poorly. Look, we had a two, sort of&nbsp;
arguably three-person team at that point.&nbsp;

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:48.060
Tim Ferriss: What does arguably three-person mean?
Elan Lee: Yeah. Well, some friends of mine would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:51.287
sort of drift in and drift&nbsp;
out in their spare time.&nbsp;

00:30:51.287 --> 00:30:53.820
Tim Ferriss: Right, right, right.
Elan Lee: I'll tell you, I learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:57.887
something amazing. So we had a Gmail account&nbsp;
associated with the Kickstarter page, and —&nbsp;

00:30:57.887 --> 00:31:02.460
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.
Elan Lee: — apparently there is a limit if a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:07.200
Gmail account receives a certain number of emails&nbsp;
over a certain number of time, a particular amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:09.720
of time, that Gmail account gets shut down.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no.&nbsp;

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:13.427
Elan Lee: It turns out that&nbsp;
number is 10,000 per second.&nbsp;

00:31:13.427 --> 00:31:16.907
Tim Ferriss: Oh, wow.
Elan Lee: And we hit that trap.&nbsp;

00:31:16.907 --> 00:31:19.500
Tim Ferriss: Holy shit.
Elan Lee: Yeah. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:24.600
Tim Ferriss: So do you just assume, you're like,&nbsp;
"All right, we're not going to read 10,000 a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:29.940
second, we assume there's some Batmans in a tub&nbsp;
in there, so fuck it. We were planning on being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:33.780
able to deliver anyways, let's just, let's do it?"
Elan Lee: All right. So you asked the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:38.700
how do you convert those casual fans into&nbsp;
super fans? The way you do that is you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:43.500
make those assumptions. You actually do need to&nbsp;
read through those emails because the conversion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:47.630
process is shining a light on them.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally. Totally.&nbsp;

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:53.820
Elan Lee: You have to send out an update&nbsp;
that says, "Look what you did." And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:59.640
have to have the pictures right there.&nbsp;
And the first draft of that update said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:05.880
"Look what happened." And we realized, whoa,&nbsp;
that's the wrong message. It has to say, "Look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:11.160
what you did," nothing to do with us, nothing to&nbsp;
do with just facts, you, you, spotlight on you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:16.080
And that's when people started to convert because&nbsp;
they're like, "Oh, alternate reality training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:19.980
we are more powerful together, we are a&nbsp;
community. Let's all keep working together."&nbsp;

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:27.720
Tim Ferriss: So you've learned a lot, and I'm&nbsp;
sure that the approach you take to distributing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:33.840
your games has changed over time since once you&nbsp;
establish yourself, it's like once you're Guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:39.240
N' Roses and you've had Appetite for Destruction,&nbsp;
the way you approach things is a little different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:48.420
right? But I've read you elsewhere warn, which I&nbsp;
thought was very astute so I'll mention it here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:53.640
and then I'd love for you to maybe flesh it out&nbsp;
a bit, warn people about stretch goals where for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:57.120
instance, they're like, "We're going to give a&nbsp;
t-shirt," and it's like, well, wait a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:02.580
how much experience do you have with man&nbsp;
manufacturing, fulfillment, SKUs, multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:09.300
sizes? And if you have a tabletop game, it's hard&nbsp;
enough to learn how to be in the tabletop business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:15.360
if you're new, let alone deciding you're going to&nbsp;
have also a t-shirt business to try to figure out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:19.920
not to mention the distraction cost.&nbsp;
Are there any other best practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:26.340
slash pitfalls that you would warn against?
Elan Lee: I haven't looked at the numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:32.640
recently, but there was a time about two or three&nbsp;
years ago when I looked at the top 10 Kickstarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:38.580
campaigns of all time. And we're always in that&nbsp;
list somewhere, people are constantly beating our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:42.060
record. They haven't beat our number of backers,&nbsp;
but certainly made more money than we have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:49.607
And I looked at the top 10, and of those 10,&nbsp;
we were the only company still in business.&nbsp;

00:33:49.607 --> 00:33:52.278
Tim Ferriss: Wow.
Elan Lee: And it's because when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.278 --> 00:33:55.140
that much money comes in it, well, sorry, before&nbsp;
that much money comes in, to earn that much money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.140 --> 00:34:00.840
there is this incredible temptation to branch&nbsp;
out and to become a t-shirt company and send out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:05.940
posters and say, "We're going to hand autograph&nbsp;
every single instance of this thing." And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:15.840
such a mistake. You are being backed to focus&nbsp;
on fulfilling one item. Just do that. Just stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:21.600
trying to give people more party favors, stop&nbsp;
trying to be more popular. The way you make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:27.000
those friends is by fulfilling the promise&nbsp;
you made to those people. And it's very rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:33.300
that people adhere to that. It's such a shame.
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to, I've just been so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.300 --> 00:34:37.860
excited to talk to you, man, I really appreciate&nbsp;
you making the time. And despite my caveat at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:41.220
beginning before we started recording, which&nbsp;
was, I think I'm having an allergic reaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.220 --> 00:34:45.660
I'm actually slowly pulling it together,&nbsp;
going from my zombification earlier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:48.047
semi-coherent. So thank you.
Elan Lee: My pleasure.&nbsp;

00:34:48.047 --> 00:34:53.460
Tim Ferriss: I want to give you credit where&nbsp;
credit is due. There are many ways I could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.460 --> 00:35:01.260
that, but top 10, to still be in the top 10 of&nbsp;
all time is remarkable for a lot of reasons, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:08.880
of which is as Kickstarter gains in popularity,&nbsp;
it is not an apples-to-apples comparison. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:15.240
the same way that if you look at, say, the top&nbsp;
100 box office earners of all time from a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:22.080
perspective, it's very hard to compare something&nbsp;
from the '50s or '60s with, say, 1990 because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:28.020
theater penetration, inflation,&nbsp;
right, that's a thing.&nbsp;

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:33.840
Prices go up. So it's astonishing that you&nbsp;
guys have had that degree of longevity. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:39.720
part of why I think you have been, and this is&nbsp;
speculation, but part of why I think you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:39.720 --> 00:35:45.360
been so successful, and not just once but multiple&nbsp;
times, is you're very good at applying positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:52.800
constraints and so I would love to know, for&nbsp;
instance, as a company, right, so it's one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:59.580
to go from a successful project to fulfilling&nbsp;
that project, and a lot of people are killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:07.500
just in that process, it's quite another to build&nbsp;
out a company that then expands, raises funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.500 --> 00:36:12.420
takes on private equity, and has done everything&nbsp;
that you guys have done, which is, it's very rare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:15.840
What are some of the constraints?&nbsp;
Because if I look at your games, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:22.140
I look at the form factor, the size, it seems like&nbsp;
there's thought put into maybe the maximum size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.140 --> 00:36:26.100
I don't know. And then I look at the complexity&nbsp;
and I'm like, okay, we're not dealing with metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:32.040
figurines. And so how do you think about&nbsp;
the parameters that you place on your games?&nbsp;

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:34.500
Elan Lee: Yeah. Oh, there's&nbsp;
so much to talk about here.&nbsp;

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:39.480
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.
Elan Lee: One of them — all right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:44.880
Okay. You open a game box, and I'm going to&nbsp;
break that down into two parts, there's the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:50.100
components, and then there's the box itself. Okay.&nbsp;
First, let's talk about the box itself because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:58.800
it's this ridiculous conundrum. If you were to go&nbsp;
and buy a new iPhone, that box is beautiful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:04.980
they spend 10 or 20, $50 building that hard&nbsp;
cardboard box and it's beautifully printed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:08.460
and it's glossy. And you know what you do? You&nbsp;
take out the iPhone and you throw the box away.&nbsp;

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:17.340
My margins on my box are so tiny that I need to&nbsp;
spend four cents, five cents building that box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:20.940
and you're going to take the game out and then&nbsp;
you're going to play it and you're going to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:28.511
it back in that box, and that box has to survive&nbsp;
20 years. It's insane, it is so hard to do. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.511 --> 00:37:30.300
the first thing to talk about is the constraint.&nbsp;
First, we have to understand play patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:36.660
we have to understand how the audience actually&nbsp;
uses this product. And most games will spend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:40.500
they'll stay within their margins. They'll say,&nbsp;
"Well, we can only spend four cents on the box, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:45.960
we're going to build it, we know the thing's going&nbsp;
to disintegrate, but so be it." We don't have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:45.960 --> 00:37:50.820
approach at all. We lose money on every box, and&nbsp;
that's cool. We're okay with that because we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:55.140
this is a lifelong commitment we're making to you.
Tim Ferriss: Now, just to be clear, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:59.630
say box, this is the packaging surrounding&nbsp;
the game, you're not saying on every game.&nbsp;

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:01.061
Elan Lee: Correct.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:38:01.061 --> 00:38:05.100
Elan Lee: Correct. Exactly right. We break it down&nbsp;
by, there's a certain amount of budget allocated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.100 --> 00:38:07.192
to each component and the box is one of those.
Tim Ferriss: Component.&nbsp;

00:38:07.192 --> 00:38:09.900
Elan Lee: Yeah. Now, so now let's look&nbsp;
at the actual components in the box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.900 --> 00:38:15.300
and I had to learn about all this stuff. I had no&nbsp;
idea that there was such a thing as card thickness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:19.020
or weight of different cardboard components. I&nbsp;
didn't know that there was such thing as, did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.020 --> 00:38:23.040
you know there's a catalog of sand timers that&nbsp;
you can look through? They're not all the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:28.500
Forget how much time passes through them, but the&nbsp;
quality of the sand, the quality of the plastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:32.700
does it have one layer or two layers? Are&nbsp;
they multiple colors? Have you dyed the sand?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:41.100
All this stuff. And we look at all of it.
And for our mantra as a company is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.940 --> 00:38:49.560
opening that box has to be a delight. Everything&nbsp;
you touch in there has to feel beautiful, it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:55.020
to feel like someone loved this thing, because&nbsp;
we're about to ask those players for a lot. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.020 --> 00:39:02.400
about to say, "Put down your phone, holy crap,&nbsp;
put down your phone, read 15 minutes worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:06.960
instructions," that's a huge ask, 15 minutes,&nbsp;
and explain it to everybody else at the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:10.380
and then try playing the game and you're probably&nbsp;
going to screw it up and read them again and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:17.280
try it again. The dropout rate is enormous, but&nbsp;
if they feel like someone really cares about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:22.740
this thing, someone really devoted time, maybe&nbsp;
it'll give us more of a chance. And so that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:26.700
the biggest constraint we place is we're like,&nbsp;
okay, just put the budget aside for a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.700 --> 00:39:32.940
know that what everybody else does not really&nbsp;
apply here. We need to make a beautiful product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:37.740
we need to make something that people fall in love&nbsp;
with as much as we are in love with it. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:41.760
the translation. Our love will equal theirs.
Tim Ferriss: So question about size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:43.667
not to get too personal.
Elan Lee: No.&nbsp;

00:39:43.667 --> 00:39:49.140
Tim Ferriss: No. But so, I would imagine that&nbsp;
with the components, there are materials cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:55.380
and that could be just a volume question, how&nbsp;
much of X material you are using. The games that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.380 --> 00:40:02.640
I have seen produced by Exploding Kittens have all&nbsp;
seemed easy to travel with. I don't know if that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:10.920
is a side effect of other design principles&nbsp;
or if that is sort of a first principle, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:14.312
do you have games that are much larger?
Elan Lee: We do.&nbsp;

00:40:14.312 --> 00:40:16.740
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Elan Lee: We do. We've tried a bunch of things. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:23.760
when we talk about size, there's a few&nbsp;
things to keep in mind. Travel is huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:27.720
we know people want to be able to transport these&nbsp;
things from place to place, bring it to a party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:33.960
bring it to a family reunion. That stuff is super&nbsp;
important. But when we put a box on a shelf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:39.300
we're fighting with all the other games on that&nbsp;
shelf. And if you think of a box as a billboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:45.600
the biggest billboard's going to win. So having&nbsp;
a smaller box is not necessarily advantageous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:49.740
there. But now let me pitch the flip side of&nbsp;
that, which is we've got to ship these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:57.152
from the manufacturing plant to a warehouse,&nbsp;
and how many boxes can you fit in a shipper?&nbsp;

00:40:57.152 --> 00:40:59.321
Tim Ferriss: Fit on a yeah, on a&nbsp;
pallet, and then in a container.&nbsp;

00:40:59.321 --> 00:41:04.200
Elan Lee: Exactly. Right. So all of that matters&nbsp;
a lot. So it's this very delicate balance. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:10.020
we've started doing, because we prefer smaller&nbsp;
boxes, we've started doing deals with retailers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:14.460
like Target and Walmart to say, "Look, we're going&nbsp;
to give you a smaller box, but let us build..."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:19.800
these are called shippers, they're&nbsp;
big cardboard frames that sit on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:23.340
shelf and then the game sits inside of it. So&nbsp;
we're trying to get the best of both worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:27.900
right? Big billboard, lots of presents on the&nbsp;
shelf, but the game is actually really small and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:29.901
convenient and you can take it with you anywhere.
Tim Ferriss: Do you guys, and I should know this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:29.901 --> 00:41:34.260
but I don't, how do your sales channels break&nbsp;
down? And we can edit this out if you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.260 --> 00:41:38.040
want to talk about it, but I'm curious,&nbsp;
in the beginning you had D2C, D2C, D2C,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:41.700
right? Direct-to-consumer, you're shipping,&nbsp;
you're handling all of that. As you become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.700 --> 00:41:48.540
more popular and particularly given the ease of&nbsp;
use of your games, the doors open up to using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:53.700
distributors and getting placement in places&nbsp;
like Walmart, Target, et cetera. What does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:58.800
that look like now for you guys versus in the&nbsp;
— what did it look like for the first three or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:03.000
four games, and what does it look like now?
Elan Lee: So first three or four games were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:08.520
100 percent D2C. All we did was sold the game&nbsp;
ourself. That's what Kickstarter basically was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:15.960
right? Then we moved into Amazon, and Amazon very&nbsp;
quickly eclipsed our ability to sell our own game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:21.720
And then the retailers, retailers went through&nbsp;
this incredible shift, they're like, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:28.320
our game sections used to have four games. It used&nbsp;
to be like, we've got Connect Four and Monopoly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:33.240
and that's about it." And they said, "Let's&nbsp;
actually build a real game section." And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:36.047
reached out to us and said, "What's it going&nbsp;
to take to get you on the shelf?" And we said —&nbsp;

00:42:36.047 --> 00:42:38.820
Tim Ferriss: Wow. Anchor tenant.
Elan Lee: Yeah, right. Seriously. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:41.207
seriously, we were — talk about&nbsp;
right place at the right time.&nbsp;

00:42:41.207 --> 00:42:42.570
Tim Ferriss: That's cool, man.
Elan Lee: Yeah, seriously.&nbsp;

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:45.660
Tim Ferriss: That's a big deal.
Elan Lee: It was huge. It was huge. And so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:51.780
got to kind of build the games departments with&nbsp;
them and to say, "Yeah, but you've got to teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:56.820
us everything, right? We don't even know what a&nbsp;
shelf is, we don't know what price to ask for, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:56.820 --> 00:43:00.720
don't know anything." And they worked with us and&nbsp;
we built it together and it was really awesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:06.420
And now I can't give you a retailer-by-retailer&nbsp;
breakdown, but I will say that retail accounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:10.680
for about 60 percent of our business now.
Tim Ferriss: What is the remaining 40 percent?&nbsp;

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:18.347
Elan Lee: It's a mix between online, hobby,&nbsp;
international, and direct to consumer.&nbsp;

00:43:18.347 --> 00:43:20.280
Tim Ferriss: What is hobby?
Elan Lee: Oh, sorry. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:23.390
I had to learn this too. So there's two —
Tim Ferriss: I mean, I know the word.&nbsp;

00:43:23.390 --> 00:43:27.600
Elan Lee: Yeah, yeah. Right, right. So hobby is&nbsp;
this generic term that covers a lot of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:38.640
It basically means a store that primarily sells&nbsp;
games. So if you think of kind of mom and pop game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:43.667
stores, they're typically where you're going to go&nbsp;
to buy more hardcore games, more strategy games.&nbsp;

00:43:43.667 --> 00:43:45.540
Tim Ferriss: For sure.
Elan Lee: But they're starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:51.720
really get into party games. So they have a lot of&nbsp;
interest in us. But while we can sell directly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:57.540
Target and Walmart, there's hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of hobby stores, so you have to work through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.540 --> 00:44:00.840
middle manager to deal with those people.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, distributor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:02.687
some type or something like that.
Elan Lee: Yeah, exactly. Yeah, yeah.&nbsp;

00:44:02.687 --> 00:44:06.660
Tim Ferriss: Okay. So this is going to strip away&nbsp;
the company for a second, because I'm curious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.160 --> 00:44:15.540
Let's just say there's something like Iron Chef&nbsp;
for tabletop games. If you want that, you can run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:16.843
with it. I'll take my customary 15 percent.
Elan Lee: Yeah, all right.&nbsp;

00:44:16.843 --> 00:44:21.540
Tim Ferriss: But so it's like the unveiling&nbsp;
of the components, let's just say, which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:28.020
be some constraints. I'm not going to give you&nbsp;
specific ones, but let's just say you yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:28.020 --> 00:44:32.640
kind of locked in a room, like old school Japanese&nbsp;
manga artists, this would happen, the publishers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:38.940
would be like, "You need to finish X number of&nbsp;
issues, we're going to keep you fed, but you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:44.160
going to be in a room until you finish," which&nbsp;
is very intense. But let's just say that you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:51.600
a week to design a game. You're&nbsp;
allowed to have some play testers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:54.767
come in for the second half of the week.
Elan Lee: Okay. Generous of you. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:44:54.767 --> 00:44:59.280
Tim Ferriss: You're welcome. I'm a very lenient&nbsp;
dictator. I'm known as the thoughtful warden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.540 --> 00:45:05.760
How would you personally approach it? You can have&nbsp;
pen and paper and all of the mock-up materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:09.660
that you need. How would you go about it?
Elan Lee: First of all, you don't need much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:16.200
more than pen and paper, honestly. That's it.&nbsp;
So the way I would go about it is I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:23.880
okay, given this, maybe these blank cards and&nbsp;
these sharpie pens, which I travel around with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.880 --> 00:45:27.780
everywhere, that is all my bag is filled with —
Tim Ferriss: And blank cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:29.683
you mean like a playing —
Elan Lee: Yeah, like poker cards.&nbsp;

00:45:29.683 --> 00:45:31.667
Tim Ferriss: — deck of cards? Yeah.
Elan Lee: But blank, white on both sides. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:45:31.667 --> 00:45:33.215
Tim Ferriss: Where do you get those?
Elan Lee: Amazon, and I order —&nbsp;

00:45:33.215 --> 00:45:34.800
Tim Ferriss: Amazon. All right.
Elan Lee: — literally, I order them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:41.040
by the pallet at this point. It's nuts, because&nbsp;
they're so cool, right? You need to design a game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:44.760
you've got the cards. You need dice, I don't have&nbsp;
dice, great, I'll take six cards, right? One, two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:47.376
three, four, five, six, shuffle them up,&nbsp;
draw a card, hey, you've got dice, right?&nbsp;

00:45:47.376 --> 00:45:49.560
Tim Ferriss: Cool. Yeah.
Elan Lee: They're everything. They're absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:55.560
everything. So the first thing I would look for&nbsp;
is what can I do with these very simple components&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.560 --> 00:46:01.740
that creates a core gameplay loop that I'm excited&nbsp;
about? And I can rattle off 12 of those in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:05.220
hour. That's honestly not the hard part.
Tim Ferriss: 12 different gameplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:07.500
loops that you're familiar with?
Elan Lee: That I'm excited about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.500 --> 00:46:13.020
that I can just invent it. They're not that&nbsp;
hard. It's like, okay, look, I'm going to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.220 --> 00:46:18.240
you a card and I'm going to keep a card and&nbsp;
whoever has the highest card wins, but first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.240 --> 00:46:22.740
we're going to have a conversation and, I'm making&nbsp;
this up as I go, I'm going to try to bluff you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:26.100
I'm going to try to get you to discard your card&nbsp;
because you don't think there's any chance you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:30.480
can win and you can still earn a little bit if&nbsp;
I can get you to fold with — right? Those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:35.160
easy. I can come up with a ton of those, right?&nbsp;
And again, you notice the crux of that was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:39.780
we're entertaining each other. The conversation is&nbsp;
actually the game, the cards don't matter. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.780 --> 00:46:45.540
just my mantra over and over and over again.
So we do that, come up with a bunch of those, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:50.400
then I need another person. I need at least one&nbsp;
person that I can show these to, play the games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:56.040
with, and I only care about one question. In fact,&nbsp;
we do all these play tests, we don't even give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.040 --> 00:47:00.660
out surveys anymore. There's only one question we&nbsp;
ask, which is, "When you finish playing the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:06.540
did you want to play again?" And a game is&nbsp;
done when 100 percent of the people say yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.800 --> 00:47:12.960
And it never starts that way, it's always a whole&nbsp;
bunch of nos, and let me tell you why and all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:18.060
stuff, but that's the only question I care about.&nbsp;
When you finished, did you want to play again?&nbsp;

00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:22.800
And if I can get a little bit of&nbsp;
positive feedback there from a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:27.180
showing a bunch of concepts too, and one of&nbsp;
them, they say, "Ooh, let's play that one again,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:32.400
then you've got to start thinking about, okay, why&nbsp;
did they want to play that again? Well, maybe they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:35.700
had so much fun, they wanted to play again. Maybe&nbsp;
they thought they could do better the second time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.540 --> 00:47:42.660
kind of a sense of mastery that maybe they could&nbsp;
achieve by playing again. And I try to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:47.700
what that thing is, what that compelling, that&nbsp;
thing that kept pulling them back in and double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:52.200
down on that, right? That's the thing I want to&nbsp;
shine most in the game, I'll start building the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:57.900
surrounding components all to emphasize that thing&nbsp;
over and over and over again. And then generously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:02.580
you let me have play testers at the end, I'll&nbsp;
start showing it to them, right? That is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.580 --> 00:48:07.800
point where I've got, my components are done,&nbsp;
my core gameplay loop is done, I've got a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:13.380
of the peripheral things that are starting to&nbsp;
get those cylinders to fire. Now let me put it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:13.380 --> 00:48:17.640
through the paces and see what people think.
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so let's say day one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:21.840
so day one, you get seated in your&nbsp;
comfortable yet not-too-comfortable cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:26.561
you have this hamster bottle full of coffee.
Elan Lee: Yeah, yeah. Good, good. I love it.&nbsp;

00:48:26.561 --> 00:48:29.700
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. You've got unlimited&nbsp;
ramen and you have a table, you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.700 --> 00:48:37.200
your blank cards, paper, sharpies. What is your&nbsp;
brainstorming, if that's the right word to use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:43.860
look like? Right? Is it questions? Is it&nbsp;
drawing cards? Visually or otherwise, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:47.820
just you sitting there staring at the wall, kind&nbsp;
of running through things, rehearsing different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.820 --> 00:48:52.680
things in your mind, what would that look like?
Elan Lee: I think the way that I approach that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:52.680 --> 00:49:01.140
is I always imagine two of me, and when I have&nbsp;
— I need to physically touch and hold things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:08.040
right? So even if the cards are blank, I do need&nbsp;
to hold six cards and shuffle six cards. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:15.660
if I'm holding them and I think, okay, me standing&nbsp;
in front of me, if I were to give you this thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:21.720
what conversation could we have? I think it's&nbsp;
a lot like script writing. I start with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:21.720 --> 00:49:26.160
conflict. What is it that's going to drive the&nbsp;
conversation? We can't be getting along, we can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:31.440
have the same motivation. Me handing this thing to&nbsp;
you better create a conflict that now we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:38.340
to try to resolve. And that's really it. I think&nbsp;
that's where all good ideas start from is here we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.340 --> 00:49:45.960
are in a room, there can be only one, so how are&nbsp;
we going to resolve that? Because here we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:50.940
staring at a bunch of blank cards.
Tim Ferriss: Looking at the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:59.160
it strikes me that you have chosen lanes very&nbsp;
well to plan. And what I mean by that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:05.160
you have not, as far as I can tell, maybe&nbsp;
you have, so correct me if I'm wrong, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:16.380
haven't gone off into the incredibly complex, $200&nbsp;
game that requires three hours to get the basics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:23.880
down, after which it requires probably 20 hours&nbsp;
of play to get to sort of intermediate level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:30.660
There are a lot of those games, and a lot of the&nbsp;
hobby shops seem to favor those games for a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:35.040
of reasons that I think are understandable.&nbsp;
I mean, the diehards love diehard products.&nbsp;

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:41.460
What other guiding principles do you have?&nbsp;
For instance, two-player versus you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.460 --> 00:50:48.540
three or four players to get started. Any&nbsp;
other elements that are integral pieces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:57.180
the DNA of your design process?
Elan Lee: This is stuff I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.180 --> 00:51:01.680
don't think often about. It just sort of&nbsp;
happens. So this is awesome. All right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.520 --> 00:51:07.860
When I was growing up, the biggest frustration&nbsp;
I had with games was, first and foremost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:07.860 --> 00:51:15.480
the instructions. I don't have the patience to&nbsp;
even read 20 minutes, let alone the seven hours to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:22.260
get into those crazy games. And so I really wanted&nbsp;
to make games that 10-year-old me would really get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.260 --> 00:51:27.420
a kick out of. And if you read, one of the things&nbsp;
I'm most proud of about our instructions is any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:31.680
of the instructions you look at, the very first&nbsp;
thing your eyes are going to be drawn to is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:36.780
box that says, "Hey, don't read these rules.&nbsp;
Reading is the worst way to learn how to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:41.940
a game. Instead, go to our website and we'll just&nbsp;
walk you through it in four minutes." And we made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:41.940 --> 00:51:43.610
a video. We have a how to play video.
Tim Ferriss: In video.&nbsp;

00:51:43.610 --> 00:51:47.220
Elan Lee: Yeah, right. And we did that for&nbsp;
every game, and I insisted on it. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:54.480
expensive, but I insist on that. Absolutely&nbsp;
insist to every game, don't read the instructions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.480 --> 00:52:00.060
even though we spent months working on the&nbsp;
instructions. So that's one hugely important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:02.640
thing to me. You should be able to pick up&nbsp;
the game in five minutes and you shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:05.880
even have to read to do it. You should&nbsp;
just sit down, watch a thing, you're done.&nbsp;

00:52:07.140 --> 00:52:12.660
Second thing is, of course, like I was talking&nbsp;
about, the game just has to be a tool set for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:12.660 --> 00:52:19.620
the other players to be entertaining. I think&nbsp;
the other ones though are a game cannot feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:26.580
fragile. And I don't mean the physical&nbsp;
components, I mean, it can't be too easy to cheat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:29.820 --> 00:52:35.460
You shouldn't ever feel motivated to outsmart&nbsp;
the game. The game needs to just say, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.460 --> 00:52:40.800
you already know you've already got everything&nbsp;
you need to play. We're just going to tweak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:44.220
the world a little bit. What if you could&nbsp;
only speak using single-syllable words?&nbsp;

00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:50.340
We're not getting involved too much. This thing&nbsp;
will always stand out no matter what you throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:56.820
at it. That's really important as well, because&nbsp;
so many games, I have a bookshelf with literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.820 --> 00:53:01.920
hundreds of games and so many of them, I&nbsp;
realized I accidentally broke the rules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:02.700 --> 00:53:09.600
The game was just too fragile. I didn't mean to,&nbsp;
it just keeps happening. And then I think also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:14.700
a game should be beautiful. You should look at&nbsp;
a game and smile. It should be a source of joy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:20.100
These things should be dopamine engines, and part&nbsp;
of that is you want to hold the thing and show it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:26.760
to all your friends. And that's actually probably&nbsp;
the actual final one, which is when you are done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:26.760 --> 00:53:31.980
playing, your first instinct, well, after "I want&nbsp;
to play again," your second instinct, I'll say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.980 --> 00:53:36.840
is, "I'm going to put this in my bag and take&nbsp;
it to my buddy's house because I am going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:38.160 --> 00:53:40.800
get a little bit of more joy&nbsp;
by sharing this with someone."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.820 --> 00:53:46.800
And the thing starts to have a life.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I'll give you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.800 --> 00:53:51.360
an anecdote on Poetry for Neanderthals. My&nbsp;
recommendation for folks, if you play this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.360 --> 00:53:56.880
first of all, your mileage may vary, right?&nbsp;
I don't believe that the whole world is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:56.880 --> 00:54:04.500
comprised of different-sized Tims. That's not the&nbsp;
case. However, my recommendation is, don't do what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.500 --> 00:54:10.620
I and my friends did, which is we decided we were&nbsp;
going to play with three people to 100 points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:10.620 --> 00:54:18.300
we had to win by a certain margin of points, and&nbsp;
that's not doing any exercise for six months and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:24.240
deciding you're going to do the leg workout from&nbsp;
Arnold Schwarzenegger from 1974. We all felt it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:28.920
the next day. We're like, wow, some portion&nbsp;
of our brain has not been used in that way.&nbsp;

00:54:28.920 --> 00:54:30.960
Elan Lee: That's amazing.
Tim Ferriss: We really put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.960 --> 00:54:36.000
it under extreme duress. But I do recommend&nbsp;
people check it out. I want to flash back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:44.220
your mention of the hundreds of games that&nbsp;
you have. As someone who was a super devoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:50.340
D&amp;D player for a long time, which may&nbsp;
seem, I'll just explain to folks, may seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:57.000
to contradict something that I named&nbsp;
as the appeal of a lot of your games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.000 --> 00:55:02.820
which is the simplicity. D&amp;D, especially when I&nbsp;
was playing — and I have First Edition Player's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.820 --> 00:55:06.900
Handbook and Dungeon Master's Guide and so on,&nbsp;
I still have all of my modules and campaigns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:15.120
and everything, is extremely in-depth. But it is&nbsp;
also one of the best games I've ever found for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:21.720
co-creating a narrative and finding&nbsp;
one another and making one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:27.360
interesting and entertaining. It&nbsp;
is just one of the great wonders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:30.120
of the world as far as I'm concerned.
Elan Lee: Would you say it enhanced your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:30.120 --> 00:55:32.191
friendships with the players?
Tim Ferriss: Oh, 100 percent.&nbsp;

00:55:32.191 --> 00:55:34.620
Elan Lee: Right.
Tim Ferriss: I mean, there were these occasional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.620 --> 00:55:40.740
flareups, as you might imagine, especially if a&nbsp;
dungeon master was in a pissy mood and decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.740 --> 00:55:48.480
really damage someone who was getting on his&nbsp;
nerves, but it was the defining, let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:48.480 --> 00:55:53.220
call it, sport of that section of my childhood.&nbsp;
Because I was a runt, I got the shit kicked out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:53.220 --> 00:55:59.340
of me constantly at school. There were a handful&nbsp;
of other kids who fit the same description and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:59.340 --> 00:56:03.480
banded together, I guess much like Stranger&nbsp;
Things but minus the aliens and everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:04.260 --> 00:56:10.500
to play D&amp;D. That was where we could shine.
But the reason I bring it up is that I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:14.340
played relatively few games. I've played&nbsp;
a lot of the classics. I've played Chess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.340 --> 00:56:20.940
I've played Backgammon, I've played Japanese&nbsp;
Chess, I've played Go, et cetera, but now sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:20.940 --> 00:56:26.820
of sidestepping my way nervously into the gaming&nbsp;
world, tabletop games have exploded. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:26.820 --> 00:56:33.000
a million different genres and what I've heard&nbsp;
in some interviews, not with you, but of game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:38.640
designers, is you have to keep up with the times.&nbsp;
You've got to watch where the ball is going. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:38.640 --> 00:56:43.980
seems like an arms race on some level, and I'm&nbsp;
skeptical of it. I'm skeptical of that because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.980 --> 00:56:47.940
it seems like you could end up chasing fads and&nbsp;
you decide, "Oh, Pokémon GO is really big. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.940 --> 00:56:52.140
are running through Central Park tripping&nbsp;
over benches." And if you do it incorrectly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:52.920 --> 00:56:59.220
you basically just fizzle because that was a&nbsp;
flash in the pan, not to say that that's true.&nbsp;

00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:06.780
I'm wondering about the hundreds of games that you&nbsp;
have. If you were to teach a three-hour class on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:06.780 --> 00:57:12.060
the periodic table of elements for games and&nbsp;
you're like, "These are some of the canonical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:19.860
games or types of games that you must be familiar&nbsp;
with, just like a chef has to know salt, oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:24.240
sweet, sour, umami," some of these basics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:34.860
what would some of the candidates be for that?
Elan Lee: Oh, okay. Oh, yeah. Okay. Stretching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:40.260
here because this is going to be — okay.&nbsp;
First of all, let me say, I will admit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:49.080
I am a fish out of water here. I've never played&nbsp;
D&amp;D literally ever. I've never played any of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:54.240
crazy 10-hour strategy games. I'm missing out&nbsp;
on that experience. I would love to have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:54.240 --> 00:58:00.360
experience. Lately I've been pretty busy, so it&nbsp;
just hasn't come up. I don't even know how to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:04.560
about this, but I will say that I have played&nbsp;
a ton of board games and a ton of card games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:12.780
and there is — you're right. There's a language,&nbsp;
there's a core ingredient list that's important.&nbsp;

00:58:13.680 --> 00:58:19.920
All right. The first one that's become really&nbsp;
popular right now — I'm sure there's like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:26.880
fancy industry term for it, but it's kind of like&nbsp;
a fill-in-the-blank game: Cards Against Humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.880 --> 00:58:33.300
What Do You Meme, Joking Hazard, Apples to Apples,&nbsp;
and the basic structure is always the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:41.100
There's a writer's room somewhere, they told&nbsp;
a whole bunch of funny jokes, they cut them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:46.560
up into pieces, and now you are going to get&nbsp;
credit for reassembling those jokes. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:46.560 --> 00:58:51.240
the basic principle, right? So, you have&nbsp;
a card, it's your turn, you play a card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:51.240 --> 00:58:56.460
everyone's got a hand of cards, you play your&nbsp;
card — I'm going to not to quote another game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:56.460 --> 00:59:01.200
so I'm going to just make up a completely&nbsp;
false game, which is, "What is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.800 --> 00:59:07.740
best thing I could wear on my head?" It's a&nbsp;
dumb example, but it illustrates the point.&nbsp;

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:14.640
Everyone else has a bunch of cards. I've got a&nbsp;
banana, a beach ball, turbine, et cetera. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:14.640 --> 00:59:17.580
all going to play a card. Whoever's turn it was&nbsp;
is going to vote on which one they thought was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:17.580 --> 00:59:22.140
the best, that person gets a point, off we go.&nbsp;
There's a bunch of games like that. They're very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.140 --> 00:59:30.120
popular right now. It's an important kind of&nbsp;
genre because it's really funny. The best ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:30.720 --> 00:59:35.100
make you laugh so hard you can barely&nbsp;
breathe. They're just incredible.&nbsp;

00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:39.720
The next one — let's see. Where should&nbsp;
we go next? Okay, the next one, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.720 --> 00:59:45.660
is called — I'm going to call it deck building.&nbsp;
And the idea is there's a whole bunch of cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:45.660 --> 00:59:51.720
each card does something very, very special&nbsp;
and powerful, but they're all community cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.720 --> 00:59:57.300
at first. You play the game by trying to remove&nbsp;
things from the community into your own private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:57.300 --> 01:00:02.940
stash so that then you can deploy them and beat&nbsp;
the other players. Every card has a power and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:07.020
weakness. You're trying to collect the best ones&nbsp;
you can in order to obliterate everybody else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:14.640
That's also a very popular, pretty standard one.
Tim Ferriss: If someone wanted to do a sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:19.790
a chef's menu tasting of these different games,&nbsp;
is there one that comes to mind that fits that?&nbsp;

01:00:19.790 --> 01:00:23.207
Elan Lee: Yeah. For that one, there's a&nbsp;
game out there called Dominion, which is —&nbsp;

01:00:23.207 --> 01:00:25.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, very popular.
Elan Lee: Yeah, that's a good one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.440 --> 01:00:33.660
to start with for deck building. Okay. Next&nbsp;
is, of course, collectible trading card games:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:33.660 --> 01:00:39.300
Pokémon, Magic: The Gathering, things like that.&nbsp;
I won't go into too much detail on that because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:39.300 --> 01:00:43.980
again, I must admit, I have never played any of&nbsp;
those. I just don't know anything about them.&nbsp;

01:00:43.980 --> 01:00:49.560
Tim Ferriss: Justin Gary, who came up earlier,&nbsp;
is a good person to look into it. I think he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.560 --> 01:00:55.080
the youngest ever national champion in Magic: The&nbsp;
Gathering. Really knows that genre inside and out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:55.080 --> 01:00:56.700
so people can check him out.
Elan Lee: Yeah, perfect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:57.360 --> 01:01:04.080
There's physical dexterity games, physical or&nbsp;
dexterity games. The idea is to play this game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.080 --> 01:01:13.320
you have to do something physical and whoever does&nbsp;
either the physical thing the best or makes it so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:13.320 --> 01:01:18.600
they can impede other people's physical progress&nbsp;
will win at those kinds of games. We have one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:24.780
out there, it's one of my favorite games ever.&nbsp;
It's called Throw Throw Burrito. It's dodgeball&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:30.240
and card games all mixed into one. You try to&nbsp;
collect cards while simultaneously throwing soft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:30.240 --> 01:01:34.680
squishy burritos at the other players. Collect&nbsp;
a card, gain a point, get hit with a burrito,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:34.680 --> 01:01:38.460
lose a point. Trying to do both those things&nbsp;
at once is where the chaos comes from.&nbsp;

01:01:40.080 --> 01:01:43.560
Let's see. I'm sure there's like&nbsp;
a ton more that I'm missing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:47.220 --> 01:01:52.260
I'm kind of glancing over games here trying to —
Tim Ferriss: If there are any games where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.260 --> 01:01:55.680
even if you can't name the&nbsp;
genre, you're like, "Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:55.680 --> 01:01:59.700
you must play this," in the same way that&nbsp;
if someone came up to me and they said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.700 --> 01:02:04.560
"I want to be a long-form non-fiction book&nbsp;
writer." Not that I would be the most qualified&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:04.560 --> 01:02:07.920
teacher of all time, but I would definitely&nbsp;
have certain books in mind where I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:13.080
"Okay, here are five or six you've got to read."
Elan Lee: There's a few games I've encountered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:13.080 --> 01:02:19.920
in my life that have felt so natural that it&nbsp;
doesn't feel like anybody invented them. It's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:19.920 --> 01:02:25.200
two plus two equals four, like nobody invented&nbsp;
that. They just realized the way to phrase it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:26.460 --> 01:02:32.220
There's a game out there called SET that I&nbsp;
believe falls perfectly into that category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:37.787
I'm not even going to describe what it is,&nbsp;
because you should just go and try this game.&nbsp;

01:02:37.787 --> 01:02:38.747
Tim Ferriss: It's a cool game.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:02:38.747 --> 01:02:41.090
Tim Ferriss: I've played&nbsp;
SET. It is a very cool game.&nbsp;

01:02:41.090 --> 01:02:46.500
Elan Lee: It's one of those games that I used&nbsp;
to play this with my sister, who's 16 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.500 --> 01:02:53.460
younger than me. When she was 10 and I was 26,&nbsp;
she would regularly kick my ass at that game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:53.460 --> 01:02:57.840
and it's because it activates this part of&nbsp;
your brain that either exists or doesn't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.840 --> 01:03:03.000
I'm sure a lot of people are just horrible&nbsp;
at it, but it is so neat to see a master&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:08.880
hit that flow state with this game. They're&nbsp;
discovering patterns that are right there in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:13.967
of everybody else, it's just they can see them and&nbsp;
you can't. It's beautiful. It's just beautiful.&nbsp;

01:03:13.967 --> 01:03:16.980
Tim Ferriss: Side note. This is not going&nbsp;
to mean anything to most people, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:17.820 --> 01:03:22.980
in my limited anecdotal experience, I would love&nbsp;
to know if I were able to have a few drinks with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:22.980 --> 01:03:27.780
the creators of this game, if they find women to&nbsp;
get into those flow states more easily, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:27.780 --> 01:03:32.930
I have, at least in my experience, seen that.&nbsp;
It raises all sorts of cool questions, I think.&nbsp;

01:03:32.930 --> 01:03:39.540
Elan Lee: Yeah. Yeah. I wonder how you describe&nbsp;
that skillset. What are the words to describe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:47.100
skill? I have no idea, but it's so beautiful to&nbsp;
watch a master. It gives me chills. Let's see. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:47.100 --> 01:03:51.060
last one I'll say is — I'm trying to find the&nbsp;
right category for something like Monopoly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.720 --> 01:03:56.147
because there's a whole genre of game where it's&nbsp;
like resource management, resource collection.&nbsp;

01:03:56.147 --> 01:04:00.060
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Trading like&nbsp;
Settlers of Catan and so on.&nbsp;

01:04:00.060 --> 01:04:08.460
Elan Lee: Let's talk about Settlers of Catan&nbsp;
for a second, because for me there is a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:13.740
interesting category there. That is one of the&nbsp;
few hardcore games I've played. I know a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:13.740 --> 01:04:19.320
people would call it not hardcore, but whatever. I&nbsp;
once challenged a group of designers. I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:25.620
"Describe to me the core gameplay loop of Settlers&nbsp;
of Catan." By the way, I don't know the proper way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:25.620 --> 01:04:28.140
to pronounce that word. Catan, Catan.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, me neither.&nbsp;

01:04:28.140 --> 01:04:29.990
Elan Lee: Okay.
Tim Ferriss: Tomato. Tomato.&nbsp;

01:04:29.990 --> 01:04:35.640
Elan Lee: Yeah. Right, right, right. "Describe&nbsp;
to me the core gameplay of Catan," and all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:35.640 --> 01:04:41.340
of them said the same thing or variations, which&nbsp;
was, "That's a game about collecting resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:41.340 --> 01:04:47.280
spending those resources to expand the fastest."&nbsp;
And I thought, that's not what that game is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:47.280 --> 01:04:52.260
That's not how I play that game. That game is&nbsp;
a social game. That game is a trading game. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:52.260 --> 01:04:58.200
don't even care about the game. All I care&nbsp;
about is my interactions and relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:58.200 --> 01:05:04.260
with the other players, because that's how you win&nbsp;
or lose. It was so fascinating to see that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:04.260 --> 01:05:08.460
are really experienced game designers. We were&nbsp;
playing a different game, completely different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:12.900
even though we both read the same rules.
Tim Ferriss: Question: were they experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:17.340
gamers — maybe people don't like that&nbsp;
term or they automatically think digital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:17.340 --> 01:05:24.420
but were they experienced game players but not&nbsp;
extremely experienced Catan or Catan players?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:24.420 --> 01:05:28.860
Because my friends who love — I'm just going to&nbsp;
say "Catan" because I'm American and like hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:28.860 --> 01:05:34.260
"A"s — they do think of it as a trading game.&nbsp;
There's a lot of game theory. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.260 --> 01:05:38.340
there's some people who won't make a trade unless&nbsp;
it clearly benefits them. But I have a friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:38.340 --> 01:05:45.180
who's very successful, and if something doesn't&nbsp;
clearly significantly hamper him in the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.180 --> 01:05:51.720
stages, he will make a trade because of the social&nbsp;
capital that he builds up the set of credit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:51.720 --> 01:05:56.580
later, asking for a trade. He's very successful.
Elan Lee: That's fair. You're right. I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:56.580 --> 01:06:03.240
that they were successful or experienced Catan&nbsp;
players, but that's great. That's really astute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:03.240 --> 01:06:07.800
I hadn't thought about it in that context. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: We may come back to the periodic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.800 --> 01:06:13.680
table of elements and this is — I'm becoming&nbsp;
immersed in this world, number one, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:18.540
I want to spend more time off of screens. I want&nbsp;
also help other people do that, and the way I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:25.069
hoping to do that is by designing a game of some&nbsp;
type or collaborating with people to build a game.&nbsp;

01:06:25.069 --> 01:06:26.520
Elan Lee: Good.
Tim Ferriss: I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:26.520 --> 01:06:30.360
think that's the only way for me to really&nbsp;
even make an attempt at trying to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.360 --> 01:06:36.590
it is to roll up my sleeves. You invoked a name&nbsp;
earlier that I want to revisit: Jordan Weisman.&nbsp;

01:06:36.590 --> 01:06:37.560
Elan Lee: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:37.560 --> 01:06:44.280
called him your mentor. Who is Jordan and&nbsp;
what are some of the lessons you've learned or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:44.280 --> 01:06:50.460
things that you have modeled from your mentor?
Elan Lee: I met Jordan Weisman at Microsoft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:50.460 --> 01:06:57.780
Jordan Weisman is one of the most famous game&nbsp;
designers ever. He designed BattleTech. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:57.780 --> 01:07:02.340
designed Crimson Skies. The list goes on and&nbsp;
on, but I met him — you know when you go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:02.880 --> 01:07:09.120
a virtual reality center and you play any of&nbsp;
these immersive games, where you're sitting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:09.120 --> 01:07:14.400
a big robot fighting against other robots? He&nbsp;
designed all of that and invented all of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:14.400 --> 01:07:19.500
and actually opened the first virtual reality&nbsp;
spaces ever in history. I met him at Microsoft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:20.280 --> 01:07:28.500
At the time, I was hired right out of college onto&nbsp;
this Xbox group and they hired me as a producer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:30.600 --> 01:07:33.900
I don't know anything about producing,&nbsp;
but they hired me. I thought, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:33.900 --> 01:07:37.440
cool. I think I work in the video game industry."
Tim Ferriss: What does that even mean? Because in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:37.440 --> 01:07:39.947
Hollywood, producer can be a million things.
Elan Lee: Yeah. Fair enough.&nbsp;

01:07:39.947 --> 01:07:42.060
Tim Ferriss: What does it mean in this context?
Elan Lee: The person in charge of a project.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:42.060 --> 01:07:46.500
They actually call it the program manager there.&nbsp;
It's the person who's responsible for schedule and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.500 --> 01:07:49.430
budget and staffing for a project.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Yeah.&nbsp;

01:07:49.430 --> 01:07:54.660
Elan Lee: They hired me right out of college&nbsp;
into this role and the creative director&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:54.660 --> 01:07:56.820
for the whole studio was this guy, Jordan.
Tim Ferriss: I'm sorry to keep interrupting. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:56.820 --> 01:08:01.320
would they hire you right out of college?
Elan Lee: Because I was a terrible, terrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:01.320 --> 01:08:09.300
terrible student. I failed out of many colleges,&nbsp;
but I had some really good internships. The star&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.300 --> 01:08:13.787
internship I got just talking my way in the&nbsp;
front door was at Industrial Light &amp; Magic.&nbsp;

01:08:13.787 --> 01:08:17.580
Tim Ferriss: Ah, you son of a bitch.
Elan Lee: Yeah, right? I got to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:17.580 --> 01:08:23.400
on Star Wars and I got to put that on&nbsp;
my resume. When I applied at Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:23.400 --> 01:08:28.140
I basically said, "Ignore my transcripts.&nbsp;
Ignore my history. Star Wars. Star Wars. Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:28.140 --> 01:08:33.660
Wars." And they said, "Sure. Yeah, come on over."
Tim Ferriss: Is it true that you are responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:37.620
for the beautiful neck of Jar Jar Binks?
Elan Lee: I like that you put in the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.620 --> 01:08:44.100
qualifier there: the neck. Yes. I did not work&nbsp;
on Jar Jar Binks; I worked on the connective&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:44.100 --> 01:08:51.960
tissue on his neck that tied the CG face&nbsp;
to the physical actor's body so that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:51.960 --> 01:08:57.120
didn't tear as he moved around. That was my job.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. You don't want any injuries in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:57.120 --> 01:09:01.980
the Star Wars universe, unless they're very&nbsp;
interesting. All right. So, you get hired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.980 --> 01:09:07.200
but you've done this internship, that shines&nbsp;
through, you get hired to be the producer?&nbsp;

01:09:07.200 --> 01:09:11.580
Elan Lee: Yeah. They put me on the Xbox.&nbsp;
They basically said, "We need six games.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:12.840 --> 01:09:19.800
Here's $200 million. Don't fuck up." That was it.
Tim Ferriss: I'm sorry to belabor this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:19.800 --> 01:09:24.360
Hold on. This is one thing — okay. $200 million&nbsp;
is a lot of money. What gave them confidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:24.360 --> 01:09:29.220
you could go from the neck of Jar Jar Binks to&nbsp;
managing $200 million? I mean, were there other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:29.220 --> 01:09:32.580
safeguards in place? That's a lot of money.
Elan Lee: They didn't give me $200 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:32.580 --> 01:09:36.710
They gave Jordan $200 million. I just —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, okay. Okay, okay, okay.&nbsp;

01:09:36.710 --> 01:09:45.120
Elan Lee: All right. So Jordan hired me. I&nbsp;
must have been working there for two weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:45.120 --> 01:09:50.700
where he pulled me aside and he said, "You're&nbsp;
a terrible producer. You're just awful."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:52.620 --> 01:10:00.960
And I said, "Yeah, I know." But to his credit,&nbsp;
I don't know why. He said, "I think you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:00.960 --> 01:10:05.820
be a really good designer, so we're going to&nbsp;
switch roles." I don't quite know what it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.820 --> 01:10:10.380
he saw in me, but he said, "I'm going to help&nbsp;
train you and you're going to help design games&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.380 --> 01:10:15.540
instead of produce them. We have very talented&nbsp;
people for that, far better than you. Let's try&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.540 --> 01:10:21.360
this." He immediately just started throwing me on&nbsp;
projects, with projects like Halo and saying like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:21.360 --> 01:10:28.980
"Go do this. Go help with this thing." It was&nbsp;
great. Obviously, the first six games for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.980 --> 01:10:35.520
Xbox that came out were great. They set the tone&nbsp;
for what the Xbox was and I got a ton of credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:39.360
again, all because of Jordan, because&nbsp;
he saw something in me and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:39.360 --> 01:10:43.380
"Go play in this arena instead."
I think maybe the best way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.380 --> 01:10:48.240
describe who Jordan is — years later,&nbsp;
we'd started a few companies together&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:51.300 --> 01:10:58.260
right at the point — when was this? This was&nbsp;
when George W. Bush had just won his second term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:00.840 --> 01:11:06.000
All my friends were upset. Everyone kept, "Oh,&nbsp;
God. The system is rigged. It's not fair. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:06.000 --> 01:11:09.000
shouldn't have won again. He was so bad the&nbsp;
first." All this stuff. I don't care what your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:13.200
political persuasions are, but a whole bunch of&nbsp;
people were just complaining. I would always ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:13.200 --> 01:11:18.900
the same question, "Well, did you vote?" And they&nbsp;
would all say, "Well, no, it doesn't matter." I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:18.900 --> 01:11:24.300
got so upset. I was like, "Oh, there's just this&nbsp;
one tool you have and you're not using your one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:24.300 --> 01:11:30.960
tool." I sat down with Jordan and he said, "This&nbsp;
reminds me a lot..." I was complaining about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.960 --> 01:11:35.640
thing. "All my friends, they're not voting and&nbsp;
they still have the audacity to complain about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:35.640 --> 01:11:46.020
it." And he said, "Okay, this reminds me a lot&nbsp;
of the story of how the US was founded. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:46.020 --> 01:11:49.740
a bunch of upset people and they eventually did&nbsp;
something about it." And he said, "You know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.740 --> 01:11:55.140
we should do?" Because it's Jordan, he said,&nbsp;
"We should start a clothing company." Right?&nbsp;

01:11:57.300 --> 01:12:01.380
I was like, "Well, I'm not following this at&nbsp;
all, but, yes. You know what? Let's go. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:01.380 --> 01:12:07.380
do it." What we did was we started this clothing&nbsp;
company, where we made these beautiful shirts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.380 --> 01:12:11.580
It was mostly a t-shirt company. We put out a&nbsp;
whole line of t-shirts. Each one was gorgeous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.180 --> 01:12:16.620
beautiful. We hired the best artists in the&nbsp;
world and just designed these shirts. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.620 --> 01:12:23.100
every shirt had hidden, inside of it, a secret&nbsp;
code or hidden message somewhere in the shirt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.100 --> 01:12:27.300
Sometimes it was right on the printing, like you&nbsp;
had to fold your shirt a certain way and get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.300 --> 01:12:30.000
graphics to line up and you could read —&nbsp;
suddenly, a secret message would appear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:36.060
But we used things like invisible inks, we use&nbsp;
things like glow-in-the dark inks, thermal inks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:36.060 --> 01:12:40.140
like get your shirt hot and suddenly a message&nbsp;
would appear. If you could figure out the message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:40.140 --> 01:12:43.860
you'd take it to the website, you enter in the&nbsp;
message, and a little movie would start playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:43.860 --> 01:12:48.840
a little two-minute clip. It was basically a TV&nbsp;
show. Every shirt had its own two-minute clip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:48.840 --> 01:12:54.360
They fit together like a TV show. It was episodic&nbsp;
and each one would end in a cliffhanger. "If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.360 --> 01:12:58.560
want to know what happens next, awesome, go&nbsp;
buy another shirt." That was our business.&nbsp;

01:12:58.560 --> 01:13:04.800
Tim Ferriss: How did he go from the prompt&nbsp;
of you being upset about voting to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.800 --> 01:13:09.180
the conceptual leapfrog move to that?
Elan Lee: Because Jordan's like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.180 --> 01:13:16.560
most brilliant guy in the world. The episodic&nbsp;
story that we were telling was about a band.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.220 --> 01:13:26.100
And the band had a very oppressive producer&nbsp;
that was stealing all their money, was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:26.100 --> 01:13:31.127
making a huge mess for them. It got so bad that&nbsp;
eventually a member of the band got murdered and —&nbsp;

01:13:31.127 --> 01:13:34.140
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that is bad.
Elan Lee: Really bad, right? Who did it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:40.740
That was the crux of the story. What we never told&nbsp;
players of this game, this clothing-based game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.740 --> 01:13:47.460
because this was Jordan's actual brilliant&nbsp;
idea, was as you progressed through this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:47.460 --> 01:13:52.500
the players figured out that this is a&nbsp;
beat for beat retelling of the founding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:52.500 --> 01:13:59.520
of the United States. Every character has a&nbsp;
historic counterpart. The bass player, Adam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:59.520 --> 01:14:05.280
was John Adams. The leader of the band, Jeff,&nbsp;
was Thomas Jefferson. And by listening to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:05.280 --> 01:14:09.720
story and piecing it all together, you're actually&nbsp;
learning the story of the founding of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:15.120
and why they did it and what they were rebelling&nbsp;
against and why they were so unhappy and why they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:22.260
created a way for them to work their way through&nbsp;
this system. I'm so proud to say — our players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:22.980 --> 01:14:26.400
millions of them, they started canvassing&nbsp;
their neighborhoods, showing up to Rock the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:26.400 --> 01:14:30.780
Vote parties, and they got — I don't remember&nbsp;
the exact number, so bear with me, but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.440 --> 01:14:36.240
hundreds of thousands of new registered&nbsp;
voters through this clothing company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:37.320 --> 01:14:41.580
That's really the beauty of Jordan is I can go&nbsp;
to him with a statement like, "None of my friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.580 --> 01:14:46.980
voted," he can say, "Let's start a clothing&nbsp;
company," and the answer is a hundred thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:46.980 --> 01:14:50.820
more people voted next time. It's so cool.
Tim Ferriss: That's amazing.&nbsp;

01:14:50.820 --> 01:14:52.860
Elan Lee: It's so cool.
Tim Ferriss: Jordan may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.860 --> 01:14:58.560
some unusual hardwiring that enables him to do&nbsp;
certain things, just like Michael Jordan may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:58.560 --> 01:15:03.240
have some unusual hardwiring that allows him to&nbsp;
jump and do certain things physically. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:03.900 --> 01:15:11.940
there are probably certain principles, certain&nbsp;
commandments that you could distill if you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.940 --> 01:15:17.940
to. If Jordan said, "Look, you can have 10 hours&nbsp;
of conversation with me and then you have to teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:17.940 --> 01:15:25.020
the 10 Commandments of Jordan Weisman to this&nbsp;
freshman class..." Or it could be junior class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:25.020 --> 01:15:29.820
whatever. Just let's assume they're serious. I'm&nbsp;
not going to ask you to name 10, but do any come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:29.820 --> 01:15:33.227
to mind whether sort of quintessential Jordan?
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:15:33.227 --> 01:15:37.124
Tim Ferriss: They don't have to be commandments.&nbsp;
They could be anything that comes to mind.&nbsp;

01:15:37.124 --> 01:15:41.040
Elan Lee: Yeah. There's a few that are immediate,&nbsp;
because he just drilled them into my head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:42.000 --> 01:15:48.540
One is "Nothing attracts a crowd like a crowd."&nbsp;
Try as hard as you want to market something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:48.540 --> 01:15:57.180
get a crowd. That's what actually matters.&nbsp;
Another one that he still says all the time is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:59.160 --> 01:16:02.460
"You've got a violin, I've got&nbsp;
a barn. Let's put on a show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:03.720 --> 01:16:10.620
Don't overthink this, just go. Tomorrow,&nbsp;
put on a show. You'll learn a lot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:10.620 --> 01:16:16.800
that's fine. You'll fail and that's fine,&nbsp;
too. Just go." Yeah, that one was — man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:16.800 --> 01:16:21.420
took me forever to figure out, but so important.
Tim Ferriss: Now, does that apply in the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:21.420 --> 01:16:25.920
of how you knew Jordan in the sense — and I've&nbsp;
heard other people say this. "You can think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:25.920 --> 01:16:30.950
it for five hours or you can test it for five&nbsp;
minutes and you're going to get the same benefit."&nbsp;

01:16:30.950 --> 01:16:36.240
Elan Lee: Yeah, that's right. Exactly.&nbsp;
Yeah. He really burned that into me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:38.100 --> 01:16:42.900
I started the alternate reality company with&nbsp;
Jordan. If I were in charge of that company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:42.900 --> 01:16:50.160
we would have been paralyzed. I would've been too&nbsp;
scared to do anything. But Jordan called up Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:50.160 --> 01:16:55.320
Spielberg and said, "We've got this company. We&nbsp;
can market your game better than anybody else&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.320 --> 01:17:01.200
in the world. You should hire us." And they&nbsp;
did. We had no product, we had no idea what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:01.200 --> 01:17:04.800
the hell we were doing, and suddenly with Steven&nbsp;
Spielberg, we built the world's first alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:04.800 --> 01:17:08.640
reality game because Jordan made a phone call.
Tim Ferriss: Hold on. Was he like croquet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:08.640 --> 01:17:12.890
partners with the Spielberg family, went to&nbsp;
the same social club? How does that happen?&nbsp;

01:17:12.890 --> 01:17:14.760
Elan Lee: All right. It was a little different.&nbsp;
I was at Microsoft — all right, so here's how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.760 --> 01:17:21.300
this story actually rolled out. I was working at&nbsp;
Microsoft doing this new designer, no idea what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:21.300 --> 01:17:31.080
the hell I'm doing, and one day Steven Spielberg&nbsp;
walks into my office. Seriously, right? He sits&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.080 --> 01:17:38.400
down and he says, "I've got to talk to you." He&nbsp;
says, "Your boss, Jordan, just paid Warner Bros.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:38.400 --> 01:17:44.520
I don't know, $600 million, whatever it&nbsp;
is, for the rights to my new movie, AI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.840 --> 01:17:54.480
Apparently your job is you've got to make four&nbsp;
video games about this movie. So I thought I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.480 --> 01:17:59.040
should sit down and chat with you about what&nbsp;
this movie is so we could get started." That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:59.040 --> 01:18:04.641
how I met Spielberg. Jordan just sent him to my&nbsp;
office and said, "Go talk to that guy." Yeah.&nbsp;

01:18:04.641 --> 01:18:07.740
Tim Ferriss: Not the worst day. Not the worst day.
Elan Lee: Yeah, yeah, yeah. Man, that was a weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:07.740 --> 01:18:12.960
place. It was so inspiring, so much fun, but&nbsp;
stuff like that would literally happen every day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:15.300 --> 01:18:21.180
The way that actually rolled out, unfortunately,&nbsp;
we built four games. We built an adventure game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:21.180 --> 01:18:26.640
we built a racing game, we built a&nbsp;
gladiatorial combat game. Jordan said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.640 --> 01:18:32.340
"Look, the thing we really have to do is we need&nbsp;
glue between these things. We need a way to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:32.340 --> 01:18:36.180
players from one game to the other game to the&nbsp;
other game. We've got this incredible narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:36.180 --> 01:18:41.340
but how do you understand how they all&nbsp;
fit together? We need the glue." So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.340 --> 01:18:47.700
we hired this incredible writer, a guy named&nbsp;
Sean Stewart, to write a story about the glue.&nbsp;

01:18:49.560 --> 01:18:56.340
He wrote it. It was a tearjerker, just this&nbsp;
incredible, incredible narrative. Then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:56.340 --> 01:19:02.040
movie was done, we finished all the games, we went&nbsp;
to the premiere. I don't know if you remember this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.040 --> 01:19:12.180
movie, but the movie is about an android boy who&nbsp;
is adopted by a human family, who eventually — all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.180 --> 01:19:16.860
this android boy wants is to be loved. He wants&nbsp;
his mother's affection, and eventually he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:16.860 --> 01:19:22.560
denied that. He is abandoned in the forest, no&nbsp;
one loves him, he's left to fend for himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:22.560 --> 01:19:28.860
he witnesses everybody die. He, in fact, witnesses&nbsp;
all of humanity go extinct and never actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:28.860 --> 01:19:34.320
achieves love. I don't know about you, but I don't&nbsp;
think anybody's walking out of that movie thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:34.320 --> 01:19:36.840
like, "I can't wait to play the Xbox game," right?
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:43.800 --> 01:19:46.020
Those people who came out and&nbsp;
they're like, "I'm really depressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.020 --> 01:19:51.230
but I'm not yet ready to kill myself. How&nbsp;
can I push this just a little further?"&nbsp;

01:19:51.230 --> 01:19:55.680
Elan Lee: Yeah. So, we went back to work&nbsp;
and we thought, "Oh, it's heartbreaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.680 --> 01:19:58.800
but we've got to cancel all of these&nbsp;
games. We just can't make any of them."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:59.640 --> 01:20:06.120
So we canceled them, but we thought like —
Tim Ferriss: No, wait. Hold on a second. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:06.120 --> 01:20:10.260
presumably when you sat down and Spielberg's&nbsp;
like, "Okay, kid, let me tell you what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:10.260 --> 01:20:16.500
movie's about," you had some insight into the&nbsp;
storyline and the arc of this movie. So did it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:16.500 --> 01:20:20.640
come as a surprise or were you just like, "Maybe&nbsp;
they'll change it. Maybe the studio will be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:20.640 --> 01:20:25.200
'Steven. Please, Steve, baby, we've&nbsp;
got to change the ending.'" To what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:25.200 --> 01:20:28.140
extent was it a surprise or were you're&nbsp;
like, "Hey, we're getting paid well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:28.140 --> 01:20:32.280
let's do our best, and then..."
Elan Lee: It was a surprise, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:32.280 --> 01:20:39.660
was intentional. This was a collaboration between&nbsp;
Spielberg and Kubrick. Spielberg adopted a Kubrick&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:39.660 --> 01:20:45.540
script and he really didn't want a lot of people&nbsp;
to know what it was going to be about or the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:45.540 --> 01:20:52.140
implementation. We did have the script, but it was&nbsp;
a version like 10 versions back, so it was very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:52.140 --> 01:20:57.060
very different. But by design, that was part&nbsp;
of their kind of surprise marketing was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.060 --> 01:20:59.520
"Nobody actually knows what this&nbsp;
is until we're ready to show you."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.060 --> 01:21:03.600
Unfortunately, we were some of those people.
Tim Ferriss: Makes it rougher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:03.600 --> 01:21:04.800
for the game designers.
Elan Lee: Yeah. Oh, man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:05.940 --> 01:21:11.400
So we have to cancel all the games. We'd&nbsp;
spent a year building these things. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.060 --> 01:21:18.960
sitting down with Jordan basically crying, just&nbsp;
like, "This is the worst. Everything we worked on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.960 --> 01:21:25.080
is getting canceled now." But we thought, "You&nbsp;
know what? Remember that glue? That was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:25.080 --> 01:21:29.340
cool. That narrative was really, really good."
Tim Ferriss: And by glue, you mean sort of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:29.340 --> 01:21:33.282
narrative connective tissue&nbsp;
that was woven by Sean?&nbsp;

01:21:33.282 --> 01:21:37.320
Elan Lee: By Sean Stewart, yeah. Yeah. It&nbsp;
was, again, this just beautiful story. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:37.320 --> 01:21:43.380
didn't have any engine associated with it.&nbsp;
It was just a few hundred script pages of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:43.380 --> 01:21:48.180
story of characters that lived in the same&nbsp;
world as AI and went through it with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.180 --> 01:21:55.140
landing from set piece to set piece to set piece,&nbsp;
which at the time were the Xbox games. We thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:55.140 --> 01:21:59.400
even if you remove the Xbox Games, this is still&nbsp;
a beautiful story. I wonder if there was a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.400 --> 01:22:06.900
to put it out into the world. I remember I was&nbsp;
sitting, again, heartbroken with Jordan. We were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.900 --> 01:22:13.140
at a sushi restaurant and in Seattle, and I was&nbsp;
saying, "Ah, there's got to be a way we can put&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:13.140 --> 01:22:19.140
out at least that story, at least that," and&nbsp;
his phone rang. He looked at me and he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:19.740 --> 01:22:25.920
"What if that was the story calling us right&nbsp;
now?" My brain just exploded. I was just like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:25.920 --> 01:22:33.180
"I have no idea what that means, but it feels&nbsp;
really significant." Then we sat down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:33.180 --> 01:22:39.240
we started building what is now known as the&nbsp;
first alternate reality game for Warner Bros.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:39.240 --> 01:22:42.900
for Spielberg, because we still had this&nbsp;
contract, we canceled all the games that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:42.900 --> 01:22:46.500
had to deliver something for marketing their&nbsp;
game, their movie, excuse me. So we decided,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:46.500 --> 01:22:50.880
"All right, what if we take this beautiful story,&nbsp;
cut it up into a million pieces, hide it all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:50.880 --> 01:22:58.140
the planet, and let players reassemble it, and the&nbsp;
finale of our story is the launch of the movie?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:58.140 --> 01:23:03.333
Which turned out to be called The Beast.
Tim Ferriss: Amazing. What a great story.&nbsp;

01:23:03.333 --> 01:23:04.190
Elan Lee: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Thank you for sharing.&nbsp;

01:23:04.190 --> 01:23:08.700
Elan Lee: That was so fun. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: You have such a broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:08.700 --> 01:23:16.020
spectrum of experience with games and interactions&nbsp;
of all different types, a human with component,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:16.020 --> 01:23:23.280
player with player. There's such a multifactorial&nbsp;
set of experience that you have. Right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.720 --> 01:23:32.580
you have this company, you have&nbsp;
many games. If you were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:34.200 --> 01:23:41.520
operate under a pen name and make a game that it&nbsp;
doesn't have to do at all with Exploding Kittens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:41.520 --> 01:23:47.280
is there some game that you're like, "God,&nbsp;
if there were a way, if I just had the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:47.280 --> 01:23:53.820
if I could have my druthers, a type of game or&nbsp;
anything?" Maybe you don't want to share it or who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:53.820 --> 01:23:57.420
knows? But is there something you're like, "God, I&nbsp;
wish this existed in the world and I would make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.420 --> 01:24:01.860
if I were just in a slightly different spot?"
Elan Lee: All right. I'm going to tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:01.860 --> 01:24:04.320
something I'm not allowed to tell&nbsp;
you, but I'm going to tell you anyway&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:05.040 --> 01:24:12.540
'cause, man, you set me right up for this&nbsp;
one, so here you go. I have two kids. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:12.540 --> 01:24:18.180
a five-year-old and a one-year-old. Last year,&nbsp;
my five-year-old was four. Of course, I tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:19.260 --> 01:24:27.780
playing games with her, I hate playing games with&nbsp;
her, hate it. It's because the games you can buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:27.780 --> 01:24:33.060
for kids are awful. They're the worst games. "If&nbsp;
I have to play Candy Land one more fucking time…"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.960 --> 01:24:39.420
So I dread it, and she's having a blast.
She's getting attention, so she loves it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:39.420 --> 01:24:43.980
She's like, "Daddy, can we play again?" I'm just&nbsp;
like, "Oh, please, no, anything, anything but play&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:43.980 --> 01:24:51.780
again." I started thinking, "This feels really&nbsp;
broken." You know when someone like Eddie Murphy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:51.780 --> 01:24:55.380
disappears for a while, and then he does a bunch&nbsp;
of kids' films and everyone's scratching their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:55.380 --> 01:24:58.200
head and they're like, "Why are you doing a bunch&nbsp;
of kids' films?" That's not what we know him for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:58.200 --> 01:25:04.920
All right. I'm about to tell you that story. I&nbsp;
started thinking about, why are kids games so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:04.920 --> 01:25:13.740
awful? What I realized is the model is wrong.&nbsp;
What if the model instead was Pixar, where —&nbsp;

01:25:13.740 --> 01:25:15.767
Tim Ferriss: I was just going to —
Elan Lee: Yeah —&nbsp;

01:25:15.767 --> 01:25:16.860
Tim Ferriss: I knew you were going to go to Pixar.
Elan Lee: Right. Right.&nbsp;

01:25:16.860 --> 01:25:18.227
Tim Ferriss: Ah, so good. So good.
Elan Lee: Right. Right?&nbsp;

01:25:18.227 --> 01:25:20.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Elan Lee: So what if the kids could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:20.280 --> 01:25:24.180
have an experience that they love and the parents&nbsp;
could have a different experience through the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:24.180 --> 01:25:25.980
tool set that they love as well?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:25:25.980 --> 01:25:34.080
Elan Lee: For the last two years, every night with&nbsp;
my daughter, we get out the paper and the stickers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:39.840
and the markers, and we just make games every&nbsp;
night. Every night. We made, I don't know, 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:39.840 --> 01:25:48.780
30 of these things, but a few of them are really&nbsp;
good, really good. She helped me design them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:48.780 --> 01:25:53.147
and we worked. We play them all the time, and&nbsp;
now she's sharing them with her friends, and —&nbsp;

01:25:53.147 --> 01:25:55.920
Tim Ferriss: I don't want to insult your&nbsp;
daughter's intelligence, but she's five&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:55.920 --> 01:25:57.960
currently, right?
Elan Lee: Yes. Yes.&nbsp;

01:25:57.960 --> 01:26:01.320
Tim Ferriss: It's quality time, so it's not&nbsp;
like you're like, "Come on, pull it together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:01.320 --> 01:26:06.820
You need to earn your keep around here.&nbsp;
What does this look like, a nonprofit?" But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:07.320 --> 01:26:09.923
how do you interact? I think this&nbsp;
would be helpful for parents —&nbsp;

01:26:09.923 --> 01:26:10.440
Elan Lee: Of course.
Tim Ferriss: — even if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:10.440 --> 01:26:13.200
don't care about game design.
Elan Lee: So my daughter is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:13.200 --> 01:26:18.600
in that phase where everything is about her.&nbsp;
She has no awareness if I'm having fun or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:18.600 --> 01:26:20.040
everything's about her.
Tim Ferriss: I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:20.040 --> 01:26:24.300
there are a lot of adults who are still there.
Elan Lee: Yeah. Yeah. Fair enough. Yeah, I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:24.300 --> 01:26:29.160
tell you stories there too. The interactions with&nbsp;
her, the game design is like, "How do I make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:29.160 --> 01:26:36.000
that she's being celebrated, that she is winning&nbsp;
a game?" But for me, what's going on in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:41.627
background is, "I cannot let her win. I have to be&nbsp;
trying my hardest and she has to beat me anyway."&nbsp;

01:26:41.627 --> 01:26:44.140
Tim Ferriss: Okay. That sounds hard.
Elan Lee: It's so hard, it's so hard.&nbsp;

01:26:44.140 --> 01:26:46.920
Tim Ferriss: Like severe, severe handicapping.
Elan Lee: Yeah. It's really hard and we went&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:46.920 --> 01:26:49.800
through a ton of games where that wasn't the&nbsp;
case, where I found, "Oh, I'm letting her win&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:49.800 --> 01:26:54.960
again. Let's scrap this thing and try something&nbsp;
else." we eventually ended up with a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:54.960 --> 01:27:00.360
games that all satisfy that criteria that&nbsp;
I don't let her win, and she wins anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:00.360 --> 01:27:05.820
that I'm having a blast, she's having a blast.&nbsp;
The best thing is, at the end of it, she says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:05.820 --> 01:27:10.980
"Can we play again?" I'm like, "Hell, yes.&nbsp;
I'm so glad you asked. Yes, let's play again."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:12.240 --> 01:27:16.260
the part I'm not allowed to tell you, but&nbsp;
I'm going to tell you anyway is this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:16.260 --> 01:27:22.727
five-year-old is going to have a handful of games&nbsp;
in retail this coming year that she helped design.&nbsp;

01:27:22.727 --> 01:27:24.527
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's so cool.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:27:24.527 --> 01:27:25.607
Tim Ferriss: That's so cool.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:27:25.607 --> 01:27:30.300
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man. Getting the dad gold star.
Elan Lee: It's so much fun.&nbsp;

01:27:30.300 --> 01:27:34.140
Tim Ferriss: When she's a teenager and she's&nbsp;
like, "I hate you, Dad," and you'll be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:34.140 --> 01:27:39.120
"Never forget what I did for you."
Elan Lee: She's got such a skewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:39.120 --> 01:27:43.620
perspective. Imagine if she thinks this is just&nbsp;
normal, "This is what all families do. You make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:43.620 --> 01:27:47.160
games and you put them in a store." That's what —
Tim Ferriss: I'm just imagining she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:47.160 --> 01:27:50.520
at the bouncy castle at her&nbsp;
friend's sixth birthday party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:50.520 --> 01:27:53.100
"Oh, yeah. What are you up to?&nbsp;
Are you having fun?" "Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:53.100 --> 01:27:56.220
I decorated a cake." She's like, "Oh, that's&nbsp;
cute. Yeah, I have three games at Walmart."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:59.460 --> 01:28:03.022
So speaking of sales, it's going to&nbsp;
be an abrupt, awkward transition —&nbsp;

01:28:03.022 --> 01:28:05.100
Elan Lee: Okay. Okay.
Tim Ferriss: — but I bookmarked something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:05.100 --> 01:28:10.680
you said earlier that I didn't want to interrupt&nbsp;
with at the time, but when you were talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:10.680 --> 01:28:17.100
the first three to four years of games coming&nbsp;
out of Exploding Kittens, direct-to-consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.700 --> 01:28:24.480
and then adding to Amazon, and you had a line that&nbsp;
was something along the lines of, "Amazon quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:24.480 --> 01:28:30.180
eclipsed our ability to sell our own games."&nbsp;
Is that because when people would search for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:30.180 --> 01:28:34.020
I'm just making this up, "Exploding Kittens,"&nbsp;
the SEO was such that people would be taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.020 --> 01:28:38.640
to Kickstarter? Most people are unfamiliar with&nbsp;
Kickstarter, therefore the conversions would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:38.640 --> 01:28:42.600
very low. But once it's on Amazon, which&nbsp;
also functions as its own search engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:42.600 --> 01:28:48.720
but happens to have very good discoverability on&nbsp;
something like Google, did that increase sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:48.720 --> 01:28:51.240
or is there something more to it?
Elan Lee: Everything you said is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.240 --> 01:28:57.180
true with one extra point, which is,&nbsp;
when a Kickstarter campaign closes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:58.920 --> 01:29:03.600
the Back This Project button&nbsp;
changes to a link to a website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.500 --> 01:29:08.820
So now there's an extra barrier to entry. You go&nbsp;
to the Kickstarter page, you click this thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:08.820 --> 01:29:12.360
it takes you through to another page. From there,&nbsp;
you've got to navigate to where the purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:12.360 --> 01:29:17.760
button is. That was too much for people. Amazon&nbsp;
is just, "Here's the buy button right here."&nbsp;

01:29:17.760 --> 01:29:23.280
Tim Ferriss: Do you have any idea, this&nbsp;
is going back to the archives, I know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:24.120 --> 01:29:29.520
but it just occurred to me that probably their&nbsp;
recommendation engine could not have hurt you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:30.060 --> 01:29:33.960
"If you like this game, you may also&nbsp;
like Exploding Kittens," but I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:33.960 --> 01:29:37.860
know if that was a rounding error or if&nbsp;
it ended up adding up to significant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:38.580 --> 01:29:42.240
additive sales. Do you have any idea?
Elan Lee: I actually don't know for sure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:42.240 --> 01:29:49.800
What I know about Amazon is when we launched,&nbsp;
there were so many stories that had come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:49.800 --> 01:29:55.260
about the phenomenal success on Kickstarter that&nbsp;
what worked to our advantage was, here's this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:55.260 --> 01:30:00.960
incredible thing, "More people backed this than&nbsp;
any other project ever, and it made all this money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:00.960 --> 01:30:06.660
and you can't have it." That was the story, 'cause&nbsp;
there was literally no way to get it. You missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:06.660 --> 01:30:15.240
the 30-day window. So when we finally said, "Hey,&nbsp;
it's available on Amazon," we had something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:15.240 --> 01:30:17.570
20,000 orders in the first hour.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.&nbsp;

01:30:17.570 --> 01:30:20.747
Elan Lee: It was just nuts because&nbsp;
suddenly it was, "All right. Well…"&nbsp;

01:30:20.747 --> 01:30:22.920
Tim Ferriss: Nothing attracts&nbsp;
a crowd like a crowd, right?&nbsp;

01:30:22.920 --> 01:30:25.380
Elan Lee: There you go. That's exactly right.
Tim Ferriss: Because the launch itself became&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:25.380 --> 01:30:27.103
a story that helped to —
Elan Lee: Huge event, yeah.&nbsp;

01:30:27.103 --> 01:30:30.000
Tim Ferriss: — further perpetuate the sales.
Elan Lee: Right. So now, we've got 20,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:34.980
orders in an hour. Now we're at the top of all&nbsp;
the search results on Amazon. You search for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:34.980 --> 01:30:41.160
word "Game," and it's all Exploding Kittens,&nbsp;
because the algorithm feeds on itself. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:42.360 --> 01:30:48.300
I cannot claim any credit for that. That worked&nbsp;
in our favor perfectly. All we did was just take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:48.300 --> 01:30:54.060
full advantage of it and just kept leveraging it.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. "Exploding Kittens, Lightning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:54.060 --> 01:30:57.337
in a bottle. Holy shit. Now what?" Right?
Elan Lee: Now what?&nbsp;

01:30:57.337 --> 01:31:02.460
Tim Ferriss: That when you build this company,&nbsp;
which we could spend dozens of hours just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:02.460 --> 01:31:04.483
discussing the company-building process —
Elan Lee: Yeah. Yeah.&nbsp;

01:31:04.483 --> 01:31:07.920
Tim Ferriss: — one of my questions for you,&nbsp;
because again, this is serving my interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:07.920 --> 01:31:13.860
'cause I'm curious in all of this, but you don't&nbsp;
necessarily have to name Voldemort and the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:13.860 --> 01:31:18.645
that you don't like, although I'm curious, maybe&nbsp;
another time over a glass of wine or something.&nbsp;

01:31:18.645 --> 01:31:20.100
Elan Lee: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:20.100 --> 01:31:25.080
success of Exploding Kittens, no doubt, you&nbsp;
had a lot of inbound from people who want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:25.080 --> 01:31:32.220
option it or adapt it for different uses in all&nbsp;
sorts of different media and formats and so on.&nbsp;

01:31:32.220 --> 01:31:37.020
As a company, probably at some point&nbsp;
there was at least a discussion of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:37.020 --> 01:31:44.820
"How can we expand this franchise without&nbsp;
damaging and maybe even strengthening our core&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:44.820 --> 01:31:51.600
business?" What might that look like?
What paths of exploration have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:51.600 --> 01:31:55.440
most fruitful or enjoyable?
Elan Lee: Oh, I like this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:55.440 --> 01:32:00.900
Okay. So we deployed the same principle after&nbsp;
Kickstarter as during Kickstarter, which was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:01.560 --> 01:32:07.200
'We've got to stay focused." For the first little&nbsp;
while, all it was, was "We must fulfill every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:07.200 --> 01:32:11.760
single order." We made a whole bunch of promises.&nbsp;
We thought we were shipping out a few thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:11.760 --> 01:32:17.940
games. We have to ship out 700,000 games our very&nbsp;
first order. That was literally our first purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.940 --> 01:32:25.500
order. It was like, "Okay. Well, that's really&nbsp;
hard." So we've got to first figure out logistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:25.500 --> 01:32:28.487
We've got to figure out warehousing. We've got&nbsp;
to figure out everything, everything, 'cause we —&nbsp;

01:32:28.487 --> 01:32:29.747
Tim Ferriss: Customer service.
Elan Lee: Right. I&nbsp;

01:32:29.747 --> 01:32:32.160
Tim Ferriss: Tracking.
Elan Lee: Seriously, I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:32.160 --> 01:32:35.160
"We're going to fulfill this out of my garage.&nbsp;
I'm going to invite a few friends over. I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:35.160 --> 01:32:47.700
order some pizza and beers and we're done." An&nbsp;
average game will sell maybe 50,000 units a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:47.700 --> 01:32:55.260
and we're doing 700,000 in 30 days. It's just&nbsp;
nuts, absolutely nuts. So the first thing was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:55.260 --> 01:33:00.240
"Stay focused." I'm so happy to say we delivered&nbsp;
every single one of those items on time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:00.960 --> 01:33:05.760
with the exception of the orders to Russia, which&nbsp;
is a whole 'nother story that makes me very upset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:07.140 --> 01:33:09.600
but we got them out there.
All right. I have to tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:09.600 --> 01:33:15.060
the Russia story, 'cause holy crap. All right.&nbsp;
So we didn't have many, we had 16, maybe 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:15.060 --> 01:33:23.460
orders in Russia, and we shipped them all out.
Then we started getting these emails from our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:23.460 --> 01:33:26.820
Russian backers saying, "I never got my game."&nbsp;
We're like, "Wait, you definitely got your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.820 --> 01:33:30.420
game. We've got the confirmation right here."&nbsp;
They said, "Nope, never got it." All of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:30.420 --> 01:33:36.720
"Nope, never got it." So I call up the customs,&nbsp;
there's a special name for it, customs in Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:36.720 --> 01:33:40.260
essentially. All of them go through this one&nbsp;
warehouse. I said, "Hey, I'm trying to track&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:40.260 --> 01:33:45.780
down these 16 orders, what's going on?" They said,&nbsp;
"Oh, yeah. Those were never delivered." I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:45.780 --> 01:33:50.640
"Well, I've got the confirmation right here that&nbsp;
they were delivered." They said, "Oh, it's illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:50.640 --> 01:33:56.280
to import games into Russia, so our policy is to&nbsp;
destroy them and then mark them as delivered."&nbsp;

01:33:56.280 --> 01:34:02.569
Tim Ferriss: Oh, wow.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:34:02.569 --> 01:34:06.120
Tim Ferriss: Illegal to import games.
Elan Lee: Yeah. I never figured&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:06.120 --> 01:34:07.800
that one out. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: There must have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:07.800 --> 01:34:13.320
been some very interesting political&nbsp;
contest going on. Can you imagine?&nbsp;

01:34:13.320 --> 01:34:17.700
Elan Lee: I can't even begin to wrap my&nbsp;
head around that one. Of all the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:17.700 --> 01:34:22.667
to consider a threat like this silly&nbsp;
little kitten-based card game. Yeah.&nbsp;

01:34:22.667 --> 01:34:25.260
Tim Ferriss: I will say, though,&nbsp;
I have heard many stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:29.040 --> 01:34:34.680
in many cases, first-time game designers getting&nbsp;
themselves into financial difficulties because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:35.760 --> 01:34:43.800
especially if they have a predominant overseas&nbsp;
backer base, when games are inspected, damaged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:43.800 --> 01:34:46.967
having holes punched through them.
Elan Lee: Right.&nbsp;

01:34:46.967 --> 01:34:51.039
Tim Ferriss: You can get yourself into an&nbsp;
accounts receivable, accounts payable problem.&nbsp;

01:34:51.039 --> 01:34:52.500
Elan Lee: It's nuts. It's such a huge problem.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:34:52.500 --> 01:34:58.980
Elan Lee: Oh, man, shipping and insurance on that&nbsp;
shipping is so expensive and everyone expects it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:58.980 --> 01:35:04.680
to be free or nearly free. When we show people the&nbsp;
bill, we're like, "Look, the game costs 20 bucks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:04.680 --> 01:35:08.100
but it costs us $150 to get that thing to you."
Tim Ferriss: Holy shit.&nbsp;

01:35:08.100 --> 01:35:11.852
Elan Lee: They're just like, "Well, I'll pay 10&nbsp;
of it, 'cause that's what shipping costs." Ugh.&nbsp;

01:35:11.852 --> 01:35:14.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Elan Lee: So it's really hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:14.340 --> 01:35:17.940
especially for new game designers.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Not that I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:17.940 --> 01:35:22.440
recommending people do this, but&nbsp;
presumably you can constrain your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:24.540 --> 01:35:28.003
geographic range on Kickstarter —
Elan Lee: We can.&nbsp;

01:35:28.003 --> 01:35:33.120
Tim Ferriss: — so that you can minimize some of&nbsp;
the complexity here. I say that this is former&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:33.120 --> 01:35:38.760
life, but I've shipped hundreds of thousands of&nbsp;
units with my first business, real business, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:38.760 --> 01:35:43.680
is a sports nutrition company. The stuff that you&nbsp;
run into, especially if something has, in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:43.680 --> 01:35:49.860
case, a reasonably high retail price, let's just&nbsp;
take Canada as a simple example, it gets shipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:49.860 --> 01:35:56.160
to Canada. Okay, fine. You might say it only&nbsp;
costs, well, I'm throwing this number out, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:56.160 --> 01:35:57.600
know what it is right now, but let's say it's $30.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:35:57.600 --> 01:36:00.960
Tim Ferriss: Okay, fine. So you figure&nbsp;
out a way to cover that. But then it lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:00.960 --> 01:36:05.040
at their door and they say, "Well,&nbsp;
based on this price, that customer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:05.820 --> 01:36:10.020
you need to pay us this additional cost —
Elan Lee: Oh, it is — yeah. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:10.020 --> 01:36:15.360
if they don't pay it at the door, they cannot&nbsp;
accept the shipment. Now you know what you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:15.360 --> 01:36:19.140
just made? You've just made an enemy,&nbsp;
because they're like, "What the fuck? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:19.140 --> 01:36:24.180
already paid you for shipping, and now I had&nbsp;
to pay this additional," and it is endless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:24.180 --> 01:36:31.080
It's endless. I hate shipping so much, so much.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So don't underestimate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:31.080 --> 01:36:35.940
challenges of shipping, folks. You really&nbsp;
need to plan in advance for how you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:35.940 --> 01:36:41.880
going to handle a lot of these eventualities.
Coming back to, now, this could be an antiquated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:41.880 --> 01:36:49.200
interview, but I was looking at some notes from&nbsp;
a past interview that you did, and I'm just going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:49.200 --> 01:36:53.100
to read here. Maybe it's changed, but "We have a&nbsp;
completely open-door policy when it comes to new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:53.100 --> 01:36:57.300
games. Designers pitch us games in person through&nbsp;
our website, via email, over Zoom every day."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:59.160 --> 01:37:02.340
You mentioned you're looking for games that&nbsp;
have a strong gameplay loop, simple enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:04.140 --> 01:37:09.021
et cetera, et cetera, some of which&nbsp;
we've discussed. Has that policy changed?&nbsp;

01:37:09.021 --> 01:37:10.620
Elan Lee: Yeah, we had to.
Tim Ferriss: That seems like you could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:10.620 --> 01:37:14.460
a tremendous amount of inbound — yeah, it had to,&nbsp;
right? Let's just say even at that time, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:16.140 --> 01:37:19.200
what are some of the other things&nbsp;
that you would be looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:19.200 --> 01:37:23.580
because there must have been other tent poles&nbsp;
you were looking for if you're getting a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:23.580 --> 01:37:24.943
of unsolicited —
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:37:24.943 --> 01:37:27.180
Tim Ferriss: — submissions.
Elan Lee: Well, I made this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:27.180 --> 01:37:33.180
really bad assumption, which was that people&nbsp;
who have game ideas have good game ideas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:35.580 --> 01:37:38.700
So the things that we got&nbsp;
through the door were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:39.960 --> 01:37:44.940
"Hey, when I was a kid, we used to play this&nbsp;
game, and I don't remember what it was called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:44.940 --> 01:37:50.460
but it involved three cards and you're trying&nbsp;
to collect four, so let's partner up on it."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:51.660 --> 01:37:57.900
I'm just like, "Okay, I can dismiss that one&nbsp;
email." But oh, my God, there's like 200 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:57.900 --> 01:38:05.400
these a day and I don't have time to do my real&nbsp;
job. So what we had to do was hire a very talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.400 --> 01:38:12.780
group of people to go out and get the games from&nbsp;
things like conventions, and there's a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:12.780 --> 01:38:18.720
inventor meetups and local meetups and things like&nbsp;
that. They filter them, they show me, honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:18.720 --> 01:38:24.660
not that many anymore, maybe 10 a month, and&nbsp;
they already know what I'm looking for. I've said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:24.660 --> 01:38:29.867
no to so many of them that now they understand&nbsp;
exactly the sort of thing I'm looking for, and —&nbsp;

01:38:29.867 --> 01:38:32.880
Tim Ferriss: What are some of those things?
Elan Lee: Yeah, it's a lot of the stuff we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:32.880 --> 01:38:37.260
discussed, honestly, the core thing that I'm&nbsp;
always looking for is, are players entertaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.260 --> 01:38:44.700
each other? That's the biggest fault I find in&nbsp;
almost everything presented to me is, "I made this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:44.700 --> 01:38:50.520
thing, it's really beautiful. The game is working&nbsp;
so hard to entertain the players." my response is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:50.520 --> 01:38:55.380
always like, "I don't care about those kinds of&nbsp;
games. I don't want a game to be in the spotlight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:55.380 --> 01:38:59.280
I want the players to be in the spotlight. Stop&nbsp;
showing me games that are working this hard,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:59.280 --> 01:39:05.400
and it's hard. It's really hard to find.
Tim Ferriss: It's a great example of focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:05.400 --> 01:39:10.080
and positive constraints. I just want&nbsp;
to underscore that, at least from my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:10.080 --> 01:39:16.500
perspective as I hear you describing this,&nbsp;
because if your charter is to create great games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:16.500 --> 01:39:23.160
you're fucked. It's just so broad. How&nbsp;
are you ever going to implement processes?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:24.420 --> 01:39:31.850
A follow-up question would be for me, at this&nbsp;
point, how many employees do you guys have?&nbsp;

01:39:31.850 --> 01:39:35.820
Elan Lee: About 100.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Is it necessary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:35.820 --> 01:39:44.220
go scouting for games? Is there a cost advantage&nbsp;
to doing that versus just whipping those geniuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:44.220 --> 01:39:48.540
in house, feeding them Twizzlers and Coke and —
Elan Lee: Yeah, that's right, on a little hamster&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:48.540 --> 01:39:55.860
wheel. Yeah. Okay. So 100 employees, let me&nbsp;
talk about what those employees are doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:55.860 --> 01:39:58.370
first of all, and why we went up to 100, right?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:39:58.370 --> 01:40:04.920
Elan Lee: Because we started with two or three,&nbsp;
and for a while we were outsourcing everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:04.920 --> 01:40:08.580
The components of the process of making a&nbsp;
game is not only, "Okay, we've got a design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:08.580 --> 01:40:12.540
we've got a PDF. Here's all the art, here's&nbsp;
the rules, done. Send it off to manufacturing."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:13.620 --> 01:40:20.220
After that, we outsourced everything. We&nbsp;
outsourced the company that manufactured&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:20.220 --> 01:40:24.960
them. We outsourced the company that would then&nbsp;
transport them from that plant to a warehouse. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.960 --> 01:40:28.920
outsourced the sales channel. We outsourced the&nbsp;
marketing channels. We outsource just absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:28.920 --> 01:40:37.260
everything. What Matt and I decided to do was to&nbsp;
just, "We've got this incredible opportunity, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:37.260 --> 01:40:42.960
if we just tried to learn from this as much as we&nbsp;
possibly can? What if we just try to be experts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:42.960 --> 01:40:48.120
everything just to see if we can, find the failure&nbsp;
points, hire smarter people to then fill those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:48.120 --> 01:40:59.220
gaps, but remove the reliance on external houses?"
So most of those 100 people are sales, marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:59.220 --> 01:41:06.180
production, logistics, distribution, accounting,&nbsp;
legal, nothing to do with the actual creative side&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:06.180 --> 01:41:13.260
of the company, because that we can do with a&nbsp;
very small team. It's all the other parts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:13.260 --> 01:41:18.600
we decided to try ourselves, failed at it, hired&nbsp;
way smarter people, and then built those teams in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:18.600 --> 01:41:26.460
house. It helps us become masters in this field.&nbsp;
It helps us not have to do what other companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:26.460 --> 01:41:31.680
do, and instead, blaze our own trail and figure&nbsp;
out, "Okay, we want to deliver..." Okay, perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:31.680 --> 01:41:36.540
example. The Exploding Kittens box is pretty&nbsp;
small. I want a huge piece of cardboard around it.&nbsp;

01:41:36.540 --> 01:41:39.420
If we had a bunch of external&nbsp;
dependencies, we'd fall into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:39.420 --> 01:41:42.900
same category with every other game. Nobody&nbsp;
gets treatment like that. That's impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:43.740 --> 01:41:48.900
But now because we're dealing with Target and&nbsp;
Walmart directly, literally calling them on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:48.900 --> 01:41:54.780
phone every day, and we deal directly with the&nbsp;
manufacturer, and we own that cardboard stock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:54.780 --> 01:42:00.300
and we own the ink to put on that cardboard, now&nbsp;
we can have really interesting conversations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:00.300 --> 01:42:05.280
them about, "What if we put this thing on your&nbsp;
shelves and we think it'll increase sales in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:05.280 --> 01:42:09.780
way? What if we put a little secret component&nbsp;
in the corner and we will do a whole marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.780 --> 01:42:15.000
campaign about people finding that?" It's a&nbsp;
kind of conversation that nobody gets to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.000 --> 01:42:21.240
in this industry. The only reason we get to&nbsp;
have it is because we are an end-to-end house&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:22.200 --> 01:42:27.120
of which very few exist in the world.
Tim Ferriss: So end-to-end house has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:27.120 --> 01:42:34.196
lot of vertical integration advantages, let's&nbsp;
say. Not risk-free, right? It takes fuel —&nbsp;

01:42:34.196 --> 01:42:36.827
Elan Lee: 100 mouths to feed,&nbsp;
amongst other things, right?&nbsp;

01:42:36.827 --> 01:42:39.900
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, 100 mouths to&nbsp;
feed, and it takes fuel to grow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:40.860 --> 01:42:49.260
For people who are unfamiliar, could you just&nbsp;
give us some milestones in terms of funding and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:49.260 --> 01:42:55.980
everything else? Because it's a rarity, as far&nbsp;
as I can tell, it's certainly not something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:55.980 --> 01:43:00.164
I've come across a lot, and I'm relatively new&nbsp;
to the space, but this does seem to stand out.&nbsp;

01:43:00.164 --> 01:43:04.260
Elan Lee: Yeah. So I've started a bunch of&nbsp;
startups. This is, I think, my fifth or sixth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:05.520 --> 01:43:11.400
Every time the milestones are always like,&nbsp;
"Hey, we got our seed round or friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:11.400 --> 01:43:16.260
family round," and, "Hey, we just got this retail&nbsp;
channel. We just got this distribution deal."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:17.040 --> 01:43:23.640
For this one, it was really weird because for&nbsp;
the first few years of the company, we owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:23.640 --> 01:43:30.720
100 percent of it and we had no one to answer to.&nbsp;
Instead of us going out saying, "Please, please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:30.720 --> 01:43:34.980
please, sell our game," we had people knocking&nbsp;
on our door saying, "Please, please, let us sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:34.980 --> 01:43:41.160
your game," so the milestones were really strange.
It wasn't, "We accomplished this thing." It was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:41.160 --> 01:43:44.940
"We allowed somebody else to do the&nbsp;
thing that they were asking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:44.940 --> 01:43:49.560
to sell our game, to distribute our game,&nbsp;
to market our game." It went that way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:49.560 --> 01:43:53.940
a long time. We had a bunch of people coming&nbsp;
in saying, "We want to invest in this thing,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:54.600 --> 01:44:01.500
and we had this incredible luxury to say, "Money&nbsp;
is the one thing we just don't need. There's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:01.500 --> 01:44:06.780
kinds of stuff we do need, but money is not it."
I remember one day, so this was maybe the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:06.780 --> 01:44:11.027
big milestone, one day, this guy,&nbsp;
Peter Chernin came to our office —&nbsp;

01:44:11.027 --> 01:44:13.080
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I know the name —
Elan Lee: — a legend in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:14.340 --> 01:44:20.700
and he said, "I really want to invest in your&nbsp;
company." We said, "Yeah, no, thanks." He said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:20.700 --> 01:44:27.360
"Hold on, just hear my pitch." He said, "You&nbsp;
are at the top of your game. You've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.360 --> 01:44:31.620
all the things you should be doing. You've&nbsp;
grown as far as you should absolutely grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:32.400 --> 01:44:37.680
but the next step is to stop being a game company&nbsp;
and start being an intellectual property company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:38.580 --> 01:44:44.580
What that means is TV shows and movies and theme&nbsp;
parks, and it turns out I'm probably the best in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:44.580 --> 01:44:51.600
the world at those things." Matt and I huddled&nbsp;
up and said, "Yeah, that's exactly right. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:51.600 --> 01:44:55.560
is a really good point, one that we had never&nbsp;
considered. We don't know how to grow beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:55.560 --> 01:45:01.080
this point. We've done this as big as we can."&nbsp;
So we took an investment from The Chernin Group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:01.080 --> 01:45:06.840
and that was a huge milestone for us.
We had never had partners. We'd never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:06.840 --> 01:45:16.620
given away anything, and suddenly we did. I would&nbsp;
say we spent 12 hours fretting that decision once&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:16.620 --> 01:45:20.760
we signed the papers thinking, "Oh, what have we&nbsp;
done? Oh, no. There's another chef in the kitchen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:20.760 --> 01:45:28.080
What does this mean?" Then Peter called us up&nbsp;
and he said, "Okay, if you could make a TV show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:28.980 --> 01:45:33.840
who would you want to make it with? Who's&nbsp;
the top people in the world?" Both of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:33.840 --> 01:45:41.400
without hesitation, we're like, "Oh, well, Mike&nbsp;
Judge and Greg Daniels." Again, those two people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:41.400 --> 01:45:46.140
are worth Googling. That's Beavis and Butt-Head,&nbsp;
King of the Hill, The Office, Office Space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:46.140 --> 01:45:52.860
like holy crap, the absolute best people in&nbsp;
the world, and we're like, "But, we know..."&nbsp;

01:45:53.640 --> 01:45:57.000
He said, he's like, "Whoa, that's a really big&nbsp;
ask." We're like, "Yeah, we know, but you asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:57.000 --> 01:46:01.800
who would be our number one choices, so there it&nbsp;
is." He said, "Okay, cool," and he got off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.800 --> 01:46:06.780
phone. Next day, he called us up and he said, "Can&nbsp;
you come over to my house this afternoon? Well, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:06.780 --> 01:46:11.460
really want to chat about this TV thing." We said,&nbsp;
"Sure." We went over to his house and sitting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:11.460 --> 01:46:16.380
his living room on the couch were Greg Daniels&nbsp;
and Mike Judge. They looked at us and they said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:16.380 --> 01:46:22.800
"Peter tells us you've got this really cool&nbsp;
game that you want to make a TV show out of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:22.800 --> 01:46:26.100
and we should be the executive producers.&nbsp;
So can you please talk to us about it?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:28.140 --> 01:46:31.980
It was at that point when Matt and I stopped —
Tim Ferriss: It's one of those "Spielberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:31.980 --> 01:46:37.080
walks into your office" moments.
Elan Lee: Yeah, right? It's just these superpowers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:37.080 --> 01:46:46.200
that some people have, being able to align with&nbsp;
them and saying, "Look, together, the sum is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:46.200 --> 01:46:52.260
greater than the individual components." Peter's&nbsp;
superpower is that networking our superpowers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:52.260 --> 01:46:58.380
we've got this really delightful IP, maybe this&nbsp;
is a chocolate and peanut butter moment. It turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:58.380 --> 01:47:00.660
out, and it really was, right? So now —
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:00.660 --> 01:47:04.680
Did you say chocolate and peanut butter?
Elan Lee: I did say chocolate and peanut butter.&nbsp;

01:47:04.680 --> 01:47:05.635
Tim Ferriss: Getting all European on me.
Elan Lee: I know. All right. All right.&nbsp;

01:47:05.635 --> 01:47:12.540
Tim Ferriss: Yes, please, continue. No, I like it.
Elan Lee: So the punchline here is now we have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.540 --> 01:47:17.580
Netflix show in development and it's&nbsp;
executive produced by Greg Daniels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:17.580 --> 01:47:23.040
Mike Judge, and Peter Chernin, and that's, holy&nbsp;
crap, we're this little startup that started in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:23.040 --> 01:47:28.740
my garage trying to raise $10,000. Now&nbsp;
we're at the absolute top of the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.740 --> 01:47:33.000
and that doesn't happen on our own. There's&nbsp;
no way we could have hit that on our own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:33.000 --> 01:47:38.160
That was a huge milestone in us realizing,&nbsp;
"It's okay to give parts of this thing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:38.940 --> 01:47:43.800
as long as we choose really wisely, as long as&nbsp;
it's people who share our vision and want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:43.800 --> 01:47:47.820
make this thing bigger than we could on our own."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So for people who are curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:47.820 --> 01:47:55.980
The Chernin Group is TCG, tcg.co/portfolio.&nbsp;
We'll give you an idea of some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:55.980 --> 01:47:59.700
properties/companies and people they've&nbsp;
invested in. There are a lot of very good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:59.700 --> 01:48:07.020
ones. I see here Food52, which I know; also,&nbsp;
MeatEater, which I know because Steven Rinella&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:07.020 --> 01:48:08.927
took me on my first ever hunt —
Elan Lee: Oh, that's awesome.&nbsp;

01:48:08.927 --> 01:48:14.220
Tim Ferriss: I'm actually in one of the episodes&nbsp;
where we went to Alaska together. Exploding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:14.220 --> 01:48:19.020
Kittens is right next to MeatEater. Then you&nbsp;
have Hodinkee, which I know because my friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:19.020 --> 01:48:24.360
Kevin Rose, used to be the CEO; Barstool&nbsp;
Sports, and it goes on and on, Rooster Teeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:24.360 --> 01:48:28.740
which I have actually been to their offices.&nbsp;
I know those guys. I don't know them well, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:28.740 --> 01:48:33.420
I've been there. Scopely, which I've actually been&nbsp;
looking at more closely recently. Hello Sunshine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:33.420 --> 01:48:38.400
which some people might recognize, Reese&nbsp;
Witherspoon. They've had very nice outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:38.400 --> 01:48:42.692
with Hello Sunshine, and it goes on and on and on.&nbsp;
So these guys, much like you, are good at focus.&nbsp;

01:48:42.692 --> 01:48:43.440
Elan Lee: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:43.440 --> 01:48:47.400
very particular types of companies. Now, I&nbsp;
don't know if this is public, it might be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:47.400 --> 01:48:51.120
but how much money did you end up raising?
Elan Lee: I don't know if it's public, but I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:51.120 --> 01:48:56.400
mind saying, we raised $40 million at that point.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Okay. So you raised $40&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:56.400 --> 01:49:03.540
million. Now this is a world, not specific&nbsp;
to The Chernin Group, although I'm in some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:03.540 --> 01:49:12.120
the same companies, but I've invested in a lot of&nbsp;
mostly seed stage or series A stage companies. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:12.840 --> 01:49:20.400
with that money comes guidance, it comes health.&nbsp;
There's certain, also, expectations that they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:20.400 --> 01:49:22.487
as professional investors.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:49:22.487 --> 01:49:25.200
Tim Ferriss: How does that play out?
Elan Lee: Okay. So first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:25.200 --> 01:49:29.100
it's important to say that 40 million is still&nbsp;
sitting in our bank account because we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:29.100 --> 01:49:33.887
didn't need it. So it's sitting there. But it&nbsp;
was important to have that as a signifier, right?&nbsp;

01:49:33.887 --> 01:49:37.320
Tim Ferriss: No, and just to say, it's&nbsp;
not so much the money but once they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:37.320 --> 01:49:41.507
a portion of the company their hope is&nbsp;
at some point to have a liquidity event.&nbsp;

01:49:41.507 --> 01:49:45.720
Elan Lee: So that was really important for me to&nbsp;
understand, is that once you take on an investor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:45.720 --> 01:49:47.760
it's not a matter of when you exit the company.&nbsp;
It's not a matter of if you exit the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:47.760 --> 01:49:52.080
it's now a matter of when you will exit&nbsp;
the company. And that took me a second to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:52.080 --> 01:49:54.900
understand, but what's really interesting, so I&nbsp;
remember, "Okay, now the Chernin group is on board&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:54.900 --> 01:50:00.240
and now we have to have board meetings." That was&nbsp;
a new one for us and our very first board meeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:00.960 --> 01:50:05.700
so one of the partners at the Chernin Group is a&nbsp;
guy named Jesse Jacobs, one of the smartest people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:05.700 --> 01:50:10.440
I've ever met. And we sat down with him, we're&nbsp;
like, "Here's our first board meeting, here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:10.440 --> 01:50:15.900
our presentation." And at the end of it, he said,&nbsp;
"That was the worst presentation I've ever seen."&nbsp;

01:50:19.620 --> 01:50:23.880
And we're like, "Okay, well, we really did&nbsp;
our best. We clearly don't know what we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:23.880 --> 01:50:29.220
doing." And so to their credit, we worked so&nbsp;
closely with him after that, he's like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:29.220 --> 01:50:34.680
you get a mulligan, we're going to do this thing&nbsp;
over, but let us train you. Let us work with you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:34.680 --> 01:50:38.880
Let us hook you up with experts. Let us show&nbsp;
you other people's board decks, and you'll see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:38.880 --> 01:50:46.980
what a healthy company looks like, because you&nbsp;
two have been incredibly lucky, and you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:46.980 --> 01:50:52.980
plan on that not lasting forever." And they were&nbsp;
totally right. It was really important for us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:52.980 --> 01:50:57.300
gain that new muscle and hire a new group of&nbsp;
people. We hired our first-ever accountant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.300 --> 01:51:01.740
we hired a CFO. That was a very new thing for us.
Tim Ferriss: Wait, wait, wait, wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:01.740 --> 01:51:02.531
wait. You didn't ha —
Elan Lee: No.&nbsp;

01:51:02.531 --> 01:51:03.720
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Well, look.&nbsp;
So wait a second. So you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:03.720 --> 01:51:06.060
going in QuickBooks. I mean, what? What's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:06.060 --> 01:51:10.740
on here if you guys don't have an accountant?
Elan Lee: Yeah. Well, I really love Google Sheets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:10.740 --> 01:51:14.340
and so I literally wrote everything myself.
Tim Ferriss: Holy shit, man. Wow.&nbsp;

01:51:14.340 --> 01:51:18.180
Elan Lee: That was our first five years.
Tim Ferriss: You must have had a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:18.180 --> 01:51:21.900
of free time for those first few years.
Elan Lee: I wouldn't call it free time. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:21.900 --> 01:51:24.900
would say I had a lot of lack of sleep.
Tim Ferriss: That's what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:24.900 --> 01:51:28.307
saying. I'm saying that sarcastically.
Elan Lee: Yeah. Fair enough. All right. Okay.&nbsp;

01:51:28.307 --> 01:51:30.180
Tim Ferriss: Okay. All right.&nbsp;
So you get your accountant.&nbsp;

01:51:30.180 --> 01:51:31.320
Elan Lee: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:31.320 --> 01:51:33.840
you start getting systems in place.
Elan Lee: Start getting systems in place, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.840 --> 01:51:38.640
we start figuring out, I think the Chernin group&nbsp;
and Jesse specifically, really started to lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:38.640 --> 01:51:44.580
the charge in terms of like, "Okay, you want to be&nbsp;
good at everything. What does that actually mean?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:44.580 --> 01:51:48.420
Who do you have to hire? What are the milestones?"&nbsp;
I remember telling them, "We want to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:48.420 --> 01:51:52.320
everything, and if we fail, we'll get help. That's&nbsp;
how we'll know what we can and can't do." And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:52.320 --> 01:51:57.120
said, "Great, what does failure mean?" And I&nbsp;
thought, "Oh, shit. I haven't thought through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:57.120 --> 01:52:03.060
that definition." And they just forced discipline&nbsp;
on our company. They didn't impose anything. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:03.060 --> 01:52:06.840
said, "Look, if you want to do it that way, we&nbsp;
will support you, but you haven't even defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:06.840 --> 01:52:13.260
your terms yet. So let's start there." And it&nbsp;
was so helpful. It was so helpful. And yeah. God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:13.260 --> 01:52:19.200
I love those guys. I've, like I said, lot of&nbsp;
startups, lot of investors. I have never sung&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:19.200 --> 01:52:23.700
an investor's praise like that before because they&nbsp;
just held our hand. They're just like, "We support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.700 --> 01:52:31.080
you. We believe in you. Let's do this together."
Tim Ferriss: Do you provide equity incentives to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:31.080 --> 01:52:35.580
employees, or is it predominantly salary-based?
Elan Lee: We provide equity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:35.580 --> 01:52:40.140
to all employees. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. And feel free to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:41.460 --> 01:52:47.940
plead the fifth or whatever the legal is on this,&nbsp;
but I'm curious because I'm deeply interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.940 --> 01:52:55.320
how startup founders navigate decisions like&nbsp;
these. What percentage of the company did they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:55.320 --> 01:53:01.620
buy in their strategic investment? Or it could be&nbsp;
a range. I'm curious what the appetite looks like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:01.620 --> 01:53:09.521
Because that also starts to inform the dynamics&nbsp;
that are at play after the investment closes.&nbsp;

01:53:09.521 --> 01:53:12.982
Elan Lee: Yeah. So I'm specifically not&nbsp;
allowed to say, unfortunately, but what I —&nbsp;

01:53:12.982 --> 01:53:14.520
Tim Ferriss: No problem.
Elan Lee: — but what I will say is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:14.520 --> 01:53:21.480
what was very important to us is any terms of&nbsp;
any deal that we sign with anybody, we remain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:21.480 --> 01:53:26.880
complete control of the company. We're interested&nbsp;
in help. We're not interested in having bosses.&nbsp;

01:53:26.880 --> 01:53:33.540
Tim Ferriss: Okay. So is it fair to say they might&nbsp;
approach it like a private equity investment, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:34.140 --> 01:53:39.480
you want to ensure that you as equity&nbsp;
holders are in a position to still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:39.480 --> 01:53:42.407
retain control of the company?
Elan Lee: That's correct. Yeah.&nbsp;

01:53:42.407 --> 01:53:46.740
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Okay. Well, so you have a tiger&nbsp;
by the tail, man. I won't push, I'm not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:46.740 --> 01:53:51.300
dig for more specifics, even though I'm super,&nbsp;
super interested personally. Because it's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:51.300 --> 01:53:54.101
part of my education too. Let me ask you this —
Elan Lee: We can talk offline. How's that?&nbsp;

01:53:54.101 --> 01:53:57.840
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Yeah, yeah, that's fine.&nbsp;
So let's say this. 10 years from now, 20 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:57.840 --> 01:54:03.660
from now, at some point you'll be like, "You know&nbsp;
what? The seasons of my life, they are changing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:04.500 --> 01:54:11.520
My daughter now has 20 more hit games&nbsp;
than I do. It's time to hang up my spurs."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:12.180 --> 01:54:20.040
When you have all the Skittles that money can buy,&nbsp;
or just you're satisfied. You've checked that box.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:22.140 --> 01:54:26.100
What do you think you'll spend your time&nbsp;
on? Let's just say you're no longer running&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:26.880 --> 01:54:28.607
a company of this size.
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:54:28.607 --> 01:54:31.578
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. What do you think you'll do?
Elan Lee: I think for me, the thing that I'm most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:31.578 --> 01:54:33.120
interested in is — all right, let me phrase&nbsp;
this actually a different way. I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:33.120 --> 01:54:38.820
through line between all the companies I've&nbsp;
ever started and that have kept my interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:38.820 --> 01:54:46.320
for the longest is empowering a community. And I&nbsp;
love doing that through story. I love doing that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:47.460 --> 01:54:53.220
I really hate it when a spotlight's&nbsp;
on me. I get shy, I get weird and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:53.220 --> 01:54:57.720
uncomfortable, but I get — okay, I'm going to&nbsp;
tell you. My proudest moment so far has been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:57.720 --> 01:55:02.340
I was at an airport and the flight was canceled&nbsp;
or it was delayed. The flight was delayed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:02.340 --> 01:55:06.180
and it was so late at night that nobody could&nbsp;
get a new flight. And so we had this terminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:06.180 --> 01:55:12.000
of all these grumpy people, everyone. They&nbsp;
were just getting on the phone trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:12.000 --> 01:55:15.120
find new things and getting turned down.
And we're all realized, we just have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:15.120 --> 01:55:20.940
sit here for four hours and it sucks. And&nbsp;
everyone was complaining, and you could just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:20.940 --> 01:55:25.560
there's this horrible negative energy. Except&nbsp;
for this one group of kids sitting on the floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:25.560 --> 01:55:31.980
in a circle just laughing their asses off. And I&nbsp;
couldn't help but go over to them and look over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:31.980 --> 01:55:34.740
the shoulders and see what they were doing. And&nbsp;
sure enough, they were playing Exploding Kittens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:34.740 --> 01:55:43.740
And it was like this unbelievably beautiful moment&nbsp;
for me. And whenever I tell people that story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:43.740 --> 01:55:48.600
they always ask, "Did you tell them who you&nbsp;
were?" And the answer is, of course not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:49.260 --> 01:55:55.260
That's the opposite of what I want.
I am so delighted that I could give them a toolset&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:55.260 --> 01:56:00.420
to laugh with each other and have this horrible&nbsp;
experience transformed for them into a positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:00.420 --> 01:56:06.360
one. And I do not want any credit for that at all.&nbsp;
I just want — I loved getting to witness that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:07.140 --> 01:56:12.360
Let's set aside for a moment the fact that&nbsp;
here's this 40-something year old guy staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:12.360 --> 01:56:18.660
at a bunch of teenagers at an airport. But for&nbsp;
me, it was a very nice moment. And so when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:18.660 --> 01:56:23.760
think about what comes next, it's like, "I want&nbsp;
more of that." I want more of tool creation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:25.080 --> 01:56:33.000
to create those just little pockets of joy. And I&nbsp;
have a lot of ideas on maybe how to do it, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:33.000 --> 01:56:38.700
don't know which one is actually going to be it.
Tim Ferriss: So someone in my family worked at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:38.700 --> 01:56:46.920
chocolate factory, and at one point — they went in&nbsp;
loving chocolate, and at some point they just had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:46.920 --> 01:56:56.940
too much chocolate, and it developed a different&nbsp;
connotation. So I'm curious how you have preserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:58.140 --> 01:57:08.820
the desire and, I don't want to say novelty, but&nbsp;
the freshness of what you just described in face&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:08.820 --> 01:57:15.660
of building a company that leads you to think&nbsp;
about games outside of having the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:15.660 --> 01:57:19.980
of sitting on the floor with your friends.
Elan Lee: I once took a skydiving class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:19.980 --> 01:57:25.020
like a week-long skydiving course, and at the&nbsp;
end of it, I asked the instructor, I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:25.020 --> 01:57:30.120
"Do you ever get bored of this?" And he said, "Do&nbsp;
you ever get bored of having sex?" And I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:31.980 --> 01:57:36.900
that's exactly it. That's how I feel about&nbsp;
games. That's how I feel about this job. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:36.900 --> 01:57:42.240
not a thing with an expiration. It's a little&nbsp;
dopamine factory for me and the people who get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:42.240 --> 01:57:46.967
to have these experiences. I don't know how&nbsp;
you get bored of that. That's just eternal.&nbsp;

01:57:46.967 --> 01:57:53.220
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. All right. Well, I suppose yet&nbsp;
another vote for more games in people's lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:55.440 --> 01:58:00.600
So Elan, we've covered a lot of ground. We've&nbsp;
certainly gone in all sorts of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:00.600 --> 01:58:05.880
directions. Is there anything else you would&nbsp;
like to add? People can find Exploding Kittens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:05.880 --> 01:58:11.460
at explodingkittens.com, and everywhere,&nbsp;
of course. And there are some social links,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:11.460 --> 01:58:17.220
but you're slowly ratcheting back on those, which&nbsp;
I think will be best for your mental health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:17.220 --> 01:58:22.440
longevity, and pretty much everything in life. Is&nbsp;
there anything else that you would like to add?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:22.440 --> 01:58:27.240
Any requests of the audience, closing comments?
Elan Lee: Something I've been thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:27.900 --> 01:58:33.840
a lot about lately is I've become enamored with&nbsp;
partnerships. I really love — like, all the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:33.840 --> 01:58:39.900
things that we're making now are collaborations&nbsp;
with somebody else way smarter than me, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:39.900 --> 01:58:46.140
really interesting perspectives. We just made this&nbsp;
game with Penn Jillette from Penn and Teller, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:46.140 --> 01:58:53.040
it's like, "What a amazing brain." I can't believe&nbsp;
I got access to that for a few months and got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:53.040 --> 01:59:02.100
play with him. And what I thought about since then&nbsp;
is we as a company, Exploding Kittens, have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:02.100 --> 01:59:08.340
really unique ability to say, "All right, smart&nbsp;
people out there, all right, people who have built&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:08.340 --> 01:59:12.660
followings and built audiences. You know what's a&nbsp;
tough statement that you can't make on your own?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:12.660 --> 01:59:20.580
'I made a game.' Because it turns out it's really&nbsp;
hard to do." And I'm really proud to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:21.180 --> 01:59:26.760
we enable that. If you have a conversation&nbsp;
with me and we go down the path for a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:26.760 --> 01:59:33.540
months, I can let you say that. And that's been&nbsp;
so satisfying lately. We're doing a few of those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:33.540 --> 01:59:41.880
and it's just been, ah, it's so fun. For a guy&nbsp;
who gets very shy in social situations, using that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:41.880 --> 01:59:49.200
as my calling card, oh man, I am so set up for&nbsp;
success now. I wish I had thought of that earlier.&nbsp;

01:59:49.200 --> 01:59:52.680
Tim Ferriss: So there are many different&nbsp;
types of dance partners. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:52.680 --> 01:59:58.440
Penn Jillette is amazing. Been on the podcast,&nbsp;
I was looking, it was I think 405. So several&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:58.440 --> 02:00:03.300
hundred conversations ago on the podcast.&nbsp;
But how do you choose? What are your ideal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:03.300 --> 02:00:08.100
dance partners? What are the characteristics?
Elan Lee: It's really simple. It's people who love&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:08.100 --> 02:00:17.160
their audiences, and maybe that sounds trite, but&nbsp;
so many of the people that I encounter do not. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:17.160 --> 02:00:23.700
your audience is a tool for you to gain money,&nbsp;
if your audience is a means to an ends — not&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:23.700 --> 02:00:29.520
interested at all. But for those who are like,&nbsp;
"I cannot help but entertain these people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:29.520 --> 02:00:33.540
if I made nothing, I would still entertain&nbsp;
these people. If I had no stage or audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:33.540 --> 02:00:39.060
I would still do this exact same thing." Oh, I&nbsp;
love those people. They speak my language. We just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:39.060 --> 02:00:44.220
go on for hours. So that's my absolute favorite.
Tim Ferriss: So if somebody's listening and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:44.220 --> 02:00:46.187
they're like, "Hey, I think that's me."
Elan Lee: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:00:46.187 --> 02:00:48.360
Tim Ferriss: What do they do?
Elan Lee: They reach out, they write&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:48.360 --> 02:00:54.540
to — let's see, what's the easiest one these days?&nbsp;
If you write to just support at Exploding Kittens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:54.540 --> 02:00:58.320
that's like this really lovely group email&nbsp;
that goes out to a whole bunch of people. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:58.320 --> 02:01:01.140
guarantee it'll make its way to me.
Tim Ferriss: All right. There you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:01.140 --> 02:01:04.800
go. You heard it. You heard it here.&nbsp;
All of those audience lovers out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:06.660 --> 02:01:11.880
and there are some good ones, I mean, there are&nbsp;
— I will agree with you that it is not everyone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:11.880 --> 02:01:16.140
I would say it's actually probably even on&nbsp;
the smaller side. Like, if your audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:16.860 --> 02:01:23.340
stops delivering any financial value to&nbsp;
you today, $0, can you derive from this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:23.340 --> 02:01:29.023
audience? Would you still engage? And I think&nbsp;
the answer for a very high percentage is no.&nbsp;

02:01:29.023 --> 02:01:33.000
Elan Lee: That's absolutely right. I believe —
Tim Ferriss: The love is conditional upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:33.000 --> 02:01:43.860
financial outcome, and I understand that if&nbsp;
you are making ends meet and having to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:45.060 --> 02:01:49.980
paying rent and sending your kids to school and&nbsp;
so on. There are necessities in life. I understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:49.980 --> 02:02:00.180
that. However, the cult of the influencer, I&nbsp;
find, to be a real amplifier of fun-house mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:00.180 --> 02:02:08.520
tendencies in human nature. I do think it's a very&nbsp;
distorting playing field in a lot of sense. Not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:08.520 --> 02:02:10.920
take this off in a strange direction, but —
Elan Lee: You're going to start a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:10.920 --> 02:02:15.000
'nother hour here. All right, go ahead.
Tim Ferriss: I know. I was just going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:15.000 --> 02:02:18.540
say that what I think folks need to be very&nbsp;
cautious of is, even if they start off with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:18.540 --> 02:02:27.900
the most sincere of intentions, if you are not&nbsp;
explicitly aware of and take steps to counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:27.900 --> 02:02:34.500
the seduction of the algorithm, you will be&nbsp;
rewarded, vis-a-vis your audience, by more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:34.500 --> 02:02:43.860
more extreme caricatures of yourself, and you will&nbsp;
therefore get propelled into becoming a mask. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:43.860 --> 02:02:48.720
seen this with dozens of people now, where they&nbsp;
used to be fairly moderate in their views, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:48.720 --> 02:02:54.540
expression, their gesticulation, their volume,&nbsp;
and now they've painted themselves into a corner.&nbsp;

02:02:54.540 --> 02:03:01.380
Sometimes they don't even realize it, where the&nbsp;
personality that their audience, in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:01.380 --> 02:03:08.520
and the platforms, have shaped them to be is what&nbsp;
they are. And that's a very precarious place to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:08.520 --> 02:03:13.680
be, especially, and you've had this experience, if&nbsp;
you're on a platform where you do not own direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:14.520 --> 02:03:19.260
means of communicating with that audience&nbsp;
and if you have your organic reach throttled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:19.260 --> 02:03:25.440
and suddenly 70 percent of your positive&nbsp;
reinforcement goes away, that is a risky business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:25.440 --> 02:03:28.200
financially and psycho-emotionally.
Elan Lee: Psycho-emotionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:28.200 --> 02:03:32.820
especially. Yeah. Oh, Tim, so well phrased. I&nbsp;
cannot — the reason that I've dropped off of most&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:32.820 --> 02:03:41.880
of social media, honestly, is because it feels&nbsp;
like that's a precarious place. It feels like it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:41.880 --> 02:03:47.040
so easy to be there for the wrong reasons. It's so&nbsp;
easy to, by nature of attracting the wrong people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:47.040 --> 02:03:53.580
let them shape who you are. Let them shape&nbsp;
the products that you make. Yeah. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:53.580 --> 02:04:00.780
see the value in it anymore. And I'd rather just&nbsp;
surround myself with fun, loving, amazing people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:00.780 --> 02:04:05.280
who are nurturing and help me make amazing things.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Man. Hey, man, That is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:05.280 --> 02:04:10.800
I hope to focus more in the upcoming year. And I&nbsp;
haven't had any social apps on my phone. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:10.800 --> 02:04:14.760
I've had one, I had reinstalled Twitter to be on&nbsp;
someone's Twitter spaces, but that'll probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:14.760 --> 02:04:19.620
come off soon. Have not had any social apps&nbsp;
on my phone for about two years now, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:20.880 --> 02:04:27.720
I do not believe that 99.999&nbsp;
percent of people out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:28.380 --> 02:04:35.280
can overcome armies of data scientists and&nbsp;
extremely effective algorithms with self-control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:35.280 --> 02:04:40.020
I don't think it's possible. Don't be that person&nbsp;
who's like, "I don't think I'll get addicted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:40.020 --> 02:04:46.010
heroin. Let me just try it a few times," right?&nbsp;
Chances are you don't have an immunity bracelet.&nbsp;

02:04:46.010 --> 02:04:46.860
Elan Lee: That's right. That's right.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Build, build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:46.860 --> 02:04:51.570
build. All right, man. Well, this has&nbsp;
been really lovely. So nice to connect —&nbsp;

02:04:51.570 --> 02:04:54.590
Elan Lee: Thank you. Such a delight.
Tim Ferriss: — and spend time together.&nbsp;

02:04:54.590 --> 02:04:55.500
Elan Lee: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:55.500 --> 02:05:02.100
really enjoyed it. And I will just say to&nbsp;
everybody listening, as per usual, we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:02.100 --> 02:05:08.040
have links to everything we discussed in the show&nbsp;
notes at tim.blog/podcast. E-L-A-N. There are no&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:08.040 --> 02:05:12.780
other guests on this podcast with that name. So&nbsp;
if you go there and search, you will find this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:12.780 --> 02:05:17.340
episode in the show notes. And until next time,&nbsp;
just be a little bit kinder than is necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:18.600 --> 02:05:26.820
build more, talk less, and have some fun,&nbsp;
folks. Get off of screens, play some games.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:26.820 --> 02:05:28.860
Thanks for tuning in.
Elan Lee: Thanks, Tim.

